WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.757 --> 00:00:06.840
I'm going to talk a little
bit about scaling laws.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.257
Originally, I think we were
going to talk about inference,

00:00:09.257 --> 00:00:11.820
but I'll take a few minutes
to start on scaling laws

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.902
and then we'll figure out
where we'll go from there.

00:00:14.902 --> 00:00:20.000
OK, so the whole point of
scaling laws is kind of--

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.190
well, to begin with, I
want you to put yourself

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:23.580
into the following scenario.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:27.830
So you have a very rich friend,
and he or she has given you

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:32.210
10,000, actually, let's say
100,000, H100s for a month.

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:35.670
And you have to build the best
open source LM that you can.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.100
So this is a somewhat hard task.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.390
And we've given you some of
the tools that you need to make

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:43.160
progress on this question.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:46.930
So you can put together your
infra team and your systems

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:47.430
people.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:50.270
And you can put together a
distributed training framework.

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:51.900
In the next
assignment after that,

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.358
you're going to put together
a great pre-training data set.

00:00:54.358 --> 00:00:56.580
And then you know all about
architectures and so on.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.470
So you kind of know you
have all the pieces.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:02.180
And so we can turn the crank
and we can run the big model.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:04.860
And in the first
couple lectures,

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.850
we talked about all the
other various decisions you

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:09.390
might make along this journey.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:10.685
Like, what's the architecture?

00:01:10.685 --> 00:01:11.810
What's the hyperparameters?

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:13.768
Like, how are you going
to do all these things.

00:01:13.768 --> 00:01:16.550
Well, I think in
some ways the answer

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:19.235
I gave you from those
early lectures was just

00:01:19.235 --> 00:01:20.610
pick what other
people have done.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.632
Just follow Llama or
whatever other models.

00:01:23.632 --> 00:01:25.340
But in a way, that's
a very boring answer

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.380
because that doesn't let
you push the frontiers.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.720
If you're in a big
frontier lab and you're

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.308
going to build the
best model, you

00:01:31.308 --> 00:01:32.850
don't want to just
copy other people.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:34.280
You want to innovate.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:38.090
So how do we innovate and
get these optimized solutions

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:39.090
in the first place?

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:42.837
So that's kind of going to
be the point of scaling laws.

00:01:42.837 --> 00:01:44.420
What we want to do
is we want to build

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:49.010
simple, predictive laws for the
behavior of language models.

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:51.920
And scaling laws are
basically this whole idea

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.000
of being able to
take small models,

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.830
scale them up, and be
able to do that in order

00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:59.310
to improve your engineering.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:02.630
So one way of thinking about
this is the old unpleasant way

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:06.450
of doing deep learning is just
train a bunch of big models,

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.549
tune your hyperparameters so
that your big models are good.

00:02:09.549 --> 00:02:12.450
That's just going to cost
tons and tons of compute.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:15.050
You can't really easily do that.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:18.410
And so I think the new
optimism and if you're

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:21.530
following a lot of these
developments on scaling,

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.390
you think of this
as, all right, we're

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:25.050
going to train a
bunch of small models,

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.240
we're going to learn a lot of
things from those small models,

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:28.907
and then we're going
to extrapolate them

00:02:28.907 --> 00:02:30.300
back up to bigger models.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.440
So we're going to take our
smallest models at the left side

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.827
of this compute
scale here, and I'm

00:02:35.827 --> 00:02:37.410
going to learn a lot
about what to do,

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:38.910
and then I'm going
to nail it in one

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:42.740
go when I build my big model.

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:44.990
And the first place
I want to start with

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:48.762
is just the history and the
background of scaling laws.

00:02:48.762 --> 00:02:51.470
And I want to contextualize this
because I think when people talk

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:53.480
about scaling
laws, often this is

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.585
done in very Messianic
like AGI terms.

00:02:57.585 --> 00:02:59.210
They're like scaling
laws just tell you

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.880
that, these amazing things
are log linear forever

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:05.060
and we will achieve
superintelligence or something.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.580
But I think scaling laws are
actually much more grounded

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.210
and have a lot of
interesting history.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:12.020
And so I'm going to start
there to try to convince you

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.030
that scaling laws
aren't necessarily

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:16.440
just fitting lines
on log log plots,

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.580
although that is a very big
part of what we're going to do.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.800
And then I'm going to do
basically very easy steps.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:25.760
I'm going to try to convince you
that at least for data, scaling

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.140
laws are very natural thing
to think about and to expect.

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:32.240
So as a person that's
kind of brought up

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.757
in statistical machine
learning, my starting point

00:03:34.757 --> 00:03:36.840
is going to be statistical
machine learning right.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.700
What is scaling laws?

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.760
In some ways scaling
laws are telling us,

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:42.470
as we increase
the amount of data

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:46.160
or we change the model size,
we expect certain behaviors out

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:47.510
of the model.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:50.910
And if you go back to something
like Machine Learning 101,

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:53.570
and if you remember your VC
dimensions and Rademacher

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:56.220
complexities and so
on, in some ways,

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.110
that's the theory
version of exactly this.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:01.100
So I have on the
top, generalization

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:03.020
bound for how the--

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:05.990
the generalization bound
for the excess risk

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:09.150
of learning amongst a
finite set of k hypotheses.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:12.200
And we see that that
should scale as 1

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.060
over square root of m.

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:15.830
In some ways, that's
a theoretical version

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:17.899
of a scaling law, where
we're making predictions

00:04:17.899 --> 00:04:22.557
about how fast our errors
should decay as a function of m.

00:04:22.557 --> 00:04:24.890
On the bottom, we might have
something a little bit more

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:26.810
exotic if we're doing
generative modeling

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:29.120
and where our generative
model is a really

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.310
flexible, non-parametric class.

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:33.080
What we might do
instead is we might

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:35.250
fit some of smooth density.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.820
So in this case,
our prediction is

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:42.410
that the L2 error of
estimating a density

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:45.980
is going to be upper bounded by
some polynomial, n to the beta

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:48.000
over 2 beta plus 1.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.750
This is what some people might
call non-parametric rates.

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:53.370
So theorists have been
thinking for a very long time

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:55.590
about how sample
size, especially,

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:56.760
should relate to error.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:59.250
This is a very classic
problem that people

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.360
have thought about in
machine learning theory.

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:04.763
But these are upper bounds, not
actual realized loss values.

00:05:04.763 --> 00:05:06.930
And really, scaling laws
are in some sense the leap,

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:09.390
from thinking about
the theoretical side,

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:13.210
of how should data and model
size relate to performance

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:15.010
and going to the
empirical side of saying,

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:18.090
actually, our bounds are bad,
but maybe we can actually

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:21.480
fit these things empirically.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:25.540
And this is a fun trivia
fact or arguable trivia fact.

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:28.320
Like, what is the first
scaling law paper?

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.370
And actually, not many
papers cite this one,

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:33.120
but I think probably
the right first scaling

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:38.287
law papers is a paper from
1993, NeurIPS from Bell Labs.

00:05:38.287 --> 00:05:40.120
And you might recognize
some of these names.

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:43.320
These are kind of theorists
and some of the people

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:46.240
that have done really classic
work in machine learning theory,

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.320
like Vapnik and Corinna
Cortes and others.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:51.960
And I've taken
excerpts because I

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:53.550
was reading this
paper actually just

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:56.550
preparing this lecture earlier.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:06:00.220
And it just struck me how ahead
of its time, in many ways,

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:01.120
this paper was right.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:03.810
It's saying, training
classifiers on large databases

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:05.648
is very computationally
demanding,

00:06:05.648 --> 00:06:08.190
and we need to figure out which
ones are good before actually

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:08.797
training them.

00:06:08.797 --> 00:06:10.380
And so what we're
going to do is we're

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:13.015
going to propose a new
predictive method that predicts

00:06:13.015 --> 00:06:15.390
how good a model is going to
be without actually training

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:16.120
the whole thing.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:19.110
And that sounds a lot
like scaling laws.

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:20.760
And you'll see this later.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:23.980
But they have a functional
form that's basically like, oh,

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:26.850
the test error of a
model is expressible

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:30.280
as some irreducible error plus
a polynomial decaying term.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:32.950
And you're like, oh, that looks
a lot like a modern scaling law.

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:35.140
And they even do
the thing where they

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:38.065
train a bunch of small
models, they fit their curves

00:06:38.065 --> 00:06:39.690
and they're like,
oh, we can accurately

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:42.640
predict the behavior of
the model further out.

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:45.540
So as with many things,
I guess, scaling laws

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:50.880
partially thought about at
Bell Labs way back when.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:54.030
And of course, there's others
that I think have thought

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:57.730
about related ideas in
scaling, not just scaling laws,

00:06:57.730 --> 00:06:59.380
but also really
the modern mindset,

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:01.818
I think, of thinking
about scaling.

00:07:01.818 --> 00:07:03.360
There's another
paper that often gets

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.410
mentioned in the history of
scaling laws on Benco and Brill,

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:10.320
who was studying, how
does the performance

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.810
of a certain kind of NLP system
scale with the amount of data?

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:17.310
And they have what looks
very often a modern scaling

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:20.080
law, log axis data
on the x-axis,

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:21.760
performance on the y-axis.

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:24.610
And they're basically
arguing, well, look,

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:26.970
we can get really dramatic
performance improvements

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:28.420
just by scaling up data.

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:29.680
It's very predictable.

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:32.490
And maybe we should
consider the trade off

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:35.130
between spending time and
money on algorithm development

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:36.670
versus just
collecting more data.

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:38.045
And you're like,
huh, that sounds

00:07:38.045 --> 00:07:40.200
a lot like what a lot of
this pre-training stuff

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:42.210
is thinking about.

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:44.730
And then, finally,
one of the things

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:47.760
that I think people have thought
about recently and in the past

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:50.170
is, is this thing
really predictable?

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:52.560
What are the right
functional forms?

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:54.953
As early as 2012,
people were really

00:07:54.953 --> 00:07:57.120
thinking about, all right,
are these things actually

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:57.910
predictable?

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:01.120
Is power law, like for
example, power 3 and power 4,

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:03.930
are those really the right
functional forms for predicting

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:06.300
the behavior of models?

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:09.330
And of course, all of
this, just to remind you,

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:11.580
is thinking about the
behavior of models

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:14.460
on the y-axis, the
capabilities, as a function

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:17.075
of the amount of data that
you have on the x-axis.

00:08:17.075 --> 00:08:18.450
So that's the
relationship that I

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:20.200
think has been really
classically studied,

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:23.640
what you might call data
scaling in all these cases.

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:28.050
And if you're interested in
the earliest large scale neural

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:30.270
scaling law paper,
that would probably

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:32.770
be Hestness et al in 2017.

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:36.000
I believe they were at Baidu
when they did this work.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:41.370
They showed that for a range
of tasks, machine translation,

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:44.670
speech, and I think
some vision tasks,

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:48.425
they showed that essentially
error rates fall as a power law.

00:08:48.425 --> 00:08:49.800
And they even have
this nice plot

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:52.530
that I really like to refer
to when people are discussing

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:54.720
scaling laws, that
really your expectations

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:56.850
should be that there's
three different regions

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:58.530
in the behavior of a model.

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:00.580
Initially, you start
out at best guess.

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:02.370
You then enter
into a region where

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:04.630
you're kind of predictably
scaling the model.

00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:05.890
That's the power law region.

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:07.680
And then there's another
asymptotic region

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.180
where you're
approaching essentially

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:11.940
the irreducible error
of your model class.

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:15.090
And I'll highlight
that, I think,

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:17.070
there's been, in
the last few years,

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:19.390
a lot of talk of new phenomena.

00:09:19.390 --> 00:09:21.660
Things like, oh,
emerging capabilities

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:23.790
or scaling compute
being a new thing,

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:26.850
or systems being
really important.

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:30.520
But have you been reading
Hestness in 2017 carefully,

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:33.560
you would have seen essentially
all of these things.

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:37.260
They say actually it's
really hard to do predictions

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:39.990
by scaling law when models
are at random performance

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:42.930
because suddenly you can
leave the random region.

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:44.830
They talk about
computational limits.

00:09:44.830 --> 00:09:47.310
Actually, if we
can scale, it means

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:49.657
actually scaling by compute
is really important.

00:09:49.657 --> 00:09:51.240
And then finally,
they even say things

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:54.600
like maybe we should do things
like quantization, because if we

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:56.190
have predictable
scaling, then that

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:58.530
means we should be willing
to pay for model accuracy

00:09:58.530 --> 00:09:59.380
with compute.

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:01.530
These are all very,
very modern ideas

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:04.090
that I think a lot of the
early scaling law papers,

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:06.940
I think, kind of understood
fairly intuitively,

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:08.860
because once you
see these plots,

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:12.100
you see that actually with
predictable resource investment,

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:14.110
you get predictable
capabilities improvements.

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:19.270
So that's in some sense the
core, not quite history,

00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:24.800
but I think context that has
really shaped scaling laws.

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:25.300
All right.

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:27.070
Any questions so
far on the context?

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:29.530
This is mainly just
kind of data scaling,

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:34.170
but I wanted to make sure
we go over it carefully.

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:36.450
Yes.

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:39.520
It's pretty natural for
there to be like scaling.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:42.780
I was wondering, is there
cases where there isn't scaling

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:44.640
where that doesn't get better?

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:49.770
Yeah, so the question was,
it's natural or maybe arguably

00:10:49.770 --> 00:10:51.110
natural to expect scaling.

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:52.860
Are there cases where
we don't get scaling

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:55.500
or we get different
kinds of scaling?

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:57.780
And I think one way
of thinking about this

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:00.300
is, if you're
measuring training loss

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:02.950
or held out versions
of training loss,

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:04.800
then I think scaling
is very natural.

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:06.870
All of classical
statistical theory

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:08.605
says, things should converge.

00:11:08.605 --> 00:11:10.230
And when they converge,
eventually they

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:13.800
will get better at some
of very asymptotic sense.

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:16.680
But we do see
non-scaling behavior.

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:20.970
There was a really interesting
competition a few years back

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:23.200
called the inverse
scaling prize,

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:25.770
where they were looking for
things that scale inversely

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:27.720
as models got better.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:29.760
And a lot of these
are very niche things.

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:32.050
Like models tend to copy better.

00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:34.090
And so if you want to
suppress copying behavior,

00:11:34.090 --> 00:11:36.090
it becomes really hard
for really strong models,

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:37.530
for example.

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:40.620
But I think one thing that
ties a lot of that together

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:44.280
is if you go really far
out of distribution, where

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:46.573
the behavior is not well
specified by the data,

00:11:46.573 --> 00:11:48.240
then you can get all
sorts of behaviors,

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:49.950
like no scaling at
all or inverse scaling

00:11:49.950 --> 00:11:50.740
or what have you.

00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:52.478
So in some sense,
you can think of this

00:11:52.478 --> 00:11:55.020
as the extension of the classic
like deep learning robustness

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:55.520
problems.

00:11:58.800 --> 00:11:59.310
Cool.

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:00.240
OK.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:05.110
So now I'm going to talk about
scaling behaviors of LLMs.

00:12:05.110 --> 00:12:07.440
Just essentially going
through several kinds

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:08.800
of empirical results.

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:11.490
I'm going to walk you
through data scaling

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:13.170
in particular and
some examples, just

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:16.330
to convince you that this is a
very natural object to expect.

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:18.360
And then we'll talk
about model size, which

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:20.405
is a different kind of a thing.

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:28.390
So scaling laws I think are
fairly well established.

00:12:28.390 --> 00:12:32.170
And they seem to appear very,
very often in many variables.

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:36.100
You see scaling in
compute on the x-axis.

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:38.040
These are all taken
from Kaplan scaling law

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:40.480
paper, which I'll refer to
extensively in this lecture.

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:42.670
So the x-axis here
is log compute.

00:12:42.670 --> 00:12:45.310
Y-axis here is log test loss.

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:47.920
And on the right, you see
similar kinds of scaling

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:49.250
both for data set size.

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:51.970
So this is the amount
of data and parameters.

00:12:51.970 --> 00:12:54.940
One subtlety I'll mention
here as I talk through this

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:57.980
is when we scale things
data set size or parameters,

00:12:57.980 --> 00:13:00.250
we're always assuming
that the other variable,

00:13:00.250 --> 00:13:02.450
in this case, if you're
scaling data set size,

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:04.360
the model size is
much, much, much bigger

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:06.260
than you can saturate
with the data set size.

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:09.770
Because obviously, if you have
way more data than parameters,

00:13:09.770 --> 00:13:11.830
eventually you're
going to asymptote out.

00:13:11.830 --> 00:13:15.730
So in all of these, we're trying
to avoid the asymptote equation.

00:13:15.730 --> 00:13:18.290
They hold in also pretty
non-standard settings.

00:13:18.290 --> 00:13:20.290
They'll hold for
downstream tasks.

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:21.740
They'll hold out
of distribution,

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:25.060
which is what's being shown
here from the Kaplan paper.

00:13:25.060 --> 00:13:28.780
And so in some ways,
power law relationships

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:32.470
seem to appear more often
than we might initially

00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:36.940
expect, especially for
these OD or other variables.

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:39.297
So I want to talk
through data scaling laws

00:13:39.297 --> 00:13:41.380
first, because I think
they're the most intuitive.

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:45.130
At the very least, I think the
theory for that is fairly clear.

00:13:45.130 --> 00:13:47.320
And to be precise,
when I say something

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:51.370
like data scaling, what I mean
is just some of simple formula

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:55.120
that maps data set size, which
I'm going to refer to as n

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:57.410
to our excess error.

00:13:57.410 --> 00:14:01.630
Excess error is the error
beyond the irreducible regime.

00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:06.710
And if you recall that figure I
referred to in Hestness, well,

00:14:06.710 --> 00:14:09.310
we are going to expect is
monotonic logistic looking

00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:10.040
curves.

00:14:10.040 --> 00:14:12.370
And really our
interest is primarily

00:14:12.370 --> 00:14:14.560
going to be in the
power law region

00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:15.937
to the irreducible error region.

00:14:15.937 --> 00:14:17.770
Of course, it's very
interesting to also ask

00:14:17.770 --> 00:14:20.230
questions about what happens
in the small data regions

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:22.210
as we leave random guessing.

00:14:22.210 --> 00:14:24.590
But that's much, much
harder to reason about,

00:14:24.590 --> 00:14:26.840
whereas I think this
right tail actually,

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:30.070
I can hopefully convince you
that this part is actually

00:14:30.070 --> 00:14:35.110
a very, very natural thing
to expect power law scaling.

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:37.315
So OK.

00:14:37.315 --> 00:14:39.950
So the first empirical
observation that we have.

00:14:39.950 --> 00:14:41.620
And this is of
the thing that I'm

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:43.780
going to convince you is
natural is when we plot

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:49.010
on the x-axis data set size
and on the y-axis test loss,

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:53.470
then on the log log plot,
model performance is linear.

00:14:53.470 --> 00:14:56.570
You might call this scale-free,
or you might call it power law.

00:14:56.570 --> 00:15:02.530
These are more
physics-oriented terminology.

00:15:02.530 --> 00:15:06.340
And this was established
by many people.

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:11.410
But you might refer to Kaplan
to see many examples of this.

00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:15.500
So I think as the previous
question brought up,

00:15:15.500 --> 00:15:17.630
we kind of expect
error to be monotone.

00:15:17.630 --> 00:15:19.840
We train on more
data, error goes down.

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:21.080
Fairly obvious.

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:24.280
The part that is less obvious
is the precise functional form

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:25.480
of this scaling.

00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:29.860
So when I say it's a power law,
it's linear in log log space.

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:31.760
And then so what is the
implication of that?

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:33.880
If something is
linear in log log

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:36.550
that means that there's
a polynomial relationship

00:15:36.550 --> 00:15:40.240
between your x-axis
and your y-axis.

00:15:40.240 --> 00:15:43.068
And y is polynomial
decay natural.

00:15:43.068 --> 00:15:45.110
Well, I'm going to walk
you through two examples.

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:47.680
And both of those are going to
result in some fairly natural

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:49.480
polynomial decay.

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:52.220
So I'm going to start with
the simplest possible example.

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:56.560
This is just going to be
even Stats 101 rather than

00:15:56.560 --> 00:15:58.040
Machine Learning 101.

00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:00.280
So what I want to do
is I want to estimate

00:16:00.280 --> 00:16:01.490
the mean of a data set.

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:05.810
And estimating the mean is a
task of estimating a parameter.

00:16:05.810 --> 00:16:08.270
I can ask for what's
the scaling law?

00:16:08.270 --> 00:16:10.270
What's the error of
my mean estimation

00:16:10.270 --> 00:16:11.690
task as a function of data?

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:12.920
So I can write that down.

00:16:12.920 --> 00:16:15.290
Well, my input comes
from a Gaussian.

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:17.270
And the task is to
estimate the average.

00:16:17.270 --> 00:16:19.310
I've written those out
in the blue box above.

00:16:19.310 --> 00:16:21.050
And what's the error.

00:16:21.050 --> 00:16:23.030
Well, by very
standard arguments,

00:16:23.030 --> 00:16:25.930
the average is going to be
also distributed as a Gaussian

00:16:25.930 --> 00:16:27.800
with the standard
deviation divided by n.

00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:29.870
So I'm going to get
sigma squared over n

00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:31.280
is my estimation error, right?

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:34.960
This is the expected squared
error of my estimate.

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:37.430
And if you look at this,
this is polynomial in n.

00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:39.515
And just to really
drive the point home,

00:16:39.515 --> 00:16:41.140
you take the log of
both sides of this,

00:16:41.140 --> 00:16:47.470
log of the error on the left and
log of n on the right hand side,

00:16:47.470 --> 00:16:49.600
I get exactly log of error
is equal to negative log

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:51.320
n plus 2 log sigma.

00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:54.410
So this is exactly the
kind of thing we expect.

00:16:54.410 --> 00:16:56.320
And we expect a
slope of 1 if we were

00:16:56.320 --> 00:17:00.760
to fit a scaling law
for mean estimation.

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:04.994
So now, equipped with this
new knowledge, you might say,

00:17:04.994 --> 00:17:06.369
all right, I'm
going to go around

00:17:06.369 --> 00:17:08.786
and I'm going to look at what
the rates are for estimating

00:17:08.786 --> 00:17:09.633
different things.

00:17:09.633 --> 00:17:11.050
And that will tell
me about what I

00:17:11.050 --> 00:17:12.890
should expect for data scaling.

00:17:12.890 --> 00:17:15.400
And so you might say, oh,
what I expect is 1 over n.

00:17:15.400 --> 00:17:18.849
You might expect 1 over square
root of n for agnostic learning

00:17:18.849 --> 00:17:19.940
and so on and so forth.

00:17:19.940 --> 00:17:22.089
So we should expect to
see some pretty nice round

00:17:22.089 --> 00:17:24.819
numbers on the slope
here of a log log plot.

00:17:24.819 --> 00:17:27.460
I should expect
to see a 1 or 0.5.

00:17:27.460 --> 00:17:29.470
What do we actually
find empirically

00:17:29.470 --> 00:17:31.220
when we look across
these papers?

00:17:31.220 --> 00:17:33.625
Just to call them
out, in Hestness

00:17:33.625 --> 00:17:38.830
and for machine translation, we
see negative 0.13, for speech,

00:17:38.830 --> 00:17:42.940
we see negative 0.3, and
for language modeling,

00:17:42.940 --> 00:17:46.460
we see an exponent
of negative 0.095.

00:17:46.460 --> 00:17:50.590
Those are all much, much
slower than the 1 over n

00:17:50.590 --> 00:17:52.840
or 1 over square root
of n rates that you

00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:56.770
might expect when you're just
fitting simple functions.

00:17:56.770 --> 00:17:58.480
So why might this be?

00:17:58.480 --> 00:17:59.000
OK.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:01.205
This will be the last
math slide of this lecture

00:18:01.205 --> 00:18:03.580
and then we can go to just
fitting lines on log log plots

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:04.640
the rest of the time.

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:07.480
But this will hopefully
drive the point home

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:10.700
of why we might see
these particular slopes.

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:12.670
So we know that neural
nets aren't just

00:18:12.670 --> 00:18:14.000
estimating the mean, right?

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:15.875
Or it's not even fitting
a linear regression.

00:18:15.875 --> 00:18:17.992
They can fit
arbitrary functions.

00:18:17.992 --> 00:18:19.450
So let's turn that
into an example,

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:20.950
and let's work
through that example.

00:18:20.950 --> 00:18:24.407
So my input is x1 through xn.

00:18:24.407 --> 00:18:26.740
So I have n samples, and I'm
going to put them uniformly

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:28.660
in the 2D unit box.

00:18:28.660 --> 00:18:30.520
And I want to
estimate some random--

00:18:30.520 --> 00:18:34.040
not random-- some arbitrary
regression function y equals f.

00:18:34.040 --> 00:18:36.340
And I'll assume f is smooth,
and so on, if you really

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:37.660
want to be precise, right?

00:18:37.660 --> 00:18:40.240
There's some regularity
conditions here.

00:18:40.240 --> 00:18:44.590
A simple approach to estimating
a regression function f is just

00:18:44.590 --> 00:18:47.270
to cut the 2D space
up into small boxes,

00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:50.650
and within each box, I can
measure the average of the y

00:18:50.650 --> 00:18:51.400
values.

00:18:51.400 --> 00:18:53.530
A very simple
non-parametric regressor

00:18:53.530 --> 00:18:55.240
is to just cut the
space up and then

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:57.130
to estimate what's
going to happen.

00:18:57.130 --> 00:19:00.490
Now, informally, if we pick,
I'm going to have square root n

00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:03.650
boxes, now each box is going to
get square root of n samples,

00:19:03.650 --> 00:19:06.650
and now my error is going to
be 1 over square root of n.

00:19:06.650 --> 00:19:09.910
And if you follow this logic
through the more dimensions,

00:19:09.910 --> 00:19:11.620
you'll see that in
D dimensions, this

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:14.510
is going to be error is equal
to n to the negative 1 over D.

00:19:14.510 --> 00:19:16.810
And then my overall
scaling, if I

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:19.180
were to take log log
plots of the whole thing

00:19:19.180 --> 00:19:22.720
is I expect a slope
of negative 1 over D.

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:24.860
And so why did I walk
you through this example?

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:26.320
I walked you
through this example

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:29.110
because if you have flexible
function classes, what people

00:19:29.110 --> 00:19:31.170
call nonparametric
function classes,

00:19:31.170 --> 00:19:33.320
you expect dimension
dependence and therefore

00:19:33.320 --> 00:19:38.250
the slope of the scaling law to
actually move much more slowly.

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:40.130
And in some sense, the
slope is telling you

00:19:40.130 --> 00:19:43.610
almost precisely kind of
the intrinsic dimensionality

00:19:43.610 --> 00:19:46.560
or the ease of
learning this task.

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:50.660
And people have argued this
more formally or more literally.

00:19:50.660 --> 00:19:54.410
There's been a several
theory/empirical papers arguing

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:58.310
that really the reason why we
get these exotic or non-standard

00:19:58.310 --> 00:20:01.100
rates of learning is that
it is closely connected

00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:03.590
to the intrinsic
dimensionality of the data

00:20:03.590 --> 00:20:06.270
and for example, the plots
of these predictions,

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:08.930
the dashed lines and
these purple circles

00:20:08.930 --> 00:20:12.050
are somewhat close,
although you don't want

00:20:12.050 --> 00:20:14.000
to read too much into
this because estimation

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:16.760
of intrinsic dimension is an
extremely difficult problem

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:21.290
and as difficult as
modeling the data overall.

00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:22.763
Oh, yes.

00:20:22.763 --> 00:20:25.430
I mean, I guess this is related
to the point you made at the end

00:20:25.430 --> 00:20:26.760
since [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:26.760 --> 00:20:30.790
But yeah, how do
you generate data

00:20:30.790 --> 00:20:34.660
that has an underlying
intrinsic dimension at all

00:20:34.660 --> 00:20:38.600
from a simulation perspective?

00:20:38.600 --> 00:20:41.380
Well if you want, for
example, to generate data,

00:20:41.380 --> 00:20:42.710
that's actually not too hard.

00:20:42.710 --> 00:20:45.765
You could write down a function
that takes in five variables,

00:20:45.765 --> 00:20:48.820
right, and then that would
be-- as long as all five

00:20:48.820 --> 00:20:51.640
of those variables like don't
cancel each other, that's

00:20:51.640 --> 00:20:53.057
a five dimensional
surface and you

00:20:53.057 --> 00:20:55.223
can add a little bit of
noise and you're good to go.

00:20:55.223 --> 00:20:57.640
The difficulty here is that
they're actually doing things

00:20:57.640 --> 00:21:01.678
like training on CIFAR, and
then they're having different--

00:21:01.678 --> 00:21:03.970
they're trying to estimate
the intrinsic dimensionality

00:21:03.970 --> 00:21:04.490
of CIFAR.

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:06.060
That's a much harder task.

00:21:09.220 --> 00:21:13.780
OK, and data scaling
laws are quite useful.

00:21:13.780 --> 00:21:17.890
I was going at this from a let
me explain to you scaling laws

00:21:17.890 --> 00:21:18.890
perspective.

00:21:18.890 --> 00:21:21.190
But you can actually
use scaling laws

00:21:21.190 --> 00:21:23.020
to do many interesting things.

00:21:23.020 --> 00:21:25.600
You can make engineering
decisions of various kinds

00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:29.950
using data scaling laws, and
people do in fact do this.

00:21:29.950 --> 00:21:33.170
For example, you
might say, well,

00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:35.660
how does data set composition
affect performance,

00:21:35.660 --> 00:21:36.980
not just data set size?

00:21:36.980 --> 00:21:41.170
Well, if you're
changing the test set,

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:43.900
Kaplan et al has a really
nice figure showing actually

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:47.210
data composition only affects
the offset, not the slope.

00:21:47.210 --> 00:21:49.150
And what that would
mean is it says

00:21:49.150 --> 00:21:51.620
if you want to pick a
really good data set,

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:53.080
you don't have to
necessarily train

00:21:53.080 --> 00:21:54.530
your models at a huge scale.

00:21:54.530 --> 00:21:57.490
You can scale them down and do
your data selection experiments

00:21:57.490 --> 00:21:59.740
on much smaller models.

00:21:59.740 --> 00:22:04.760
And the shape of the expected
of as we mix different data,

00:22:04.760 --> 00:22:07.157
we might expect certain
kinds of shapes.

00:22:07.157 --> 00:22:09.490
And you can use regression
and other kinds of techniques

00:22:09.490 --> 00:22:11.980
to try to figure out, for
example, optimal data mixing

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:13.370
using scaling laws.

00:22:13.370 --> 00:22:16.610
And people have written
several papers on this topic,

00:22:16.610 --> 00:22:20.660
although as with all
data selection research,

00:22:20.660 --> 00:22:26.320
a lot of this seems fairly
tricky to execute reliably.

00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:29.320
There's other also interesting
questions that you might ask.

00:22:29.320 --> 00:22:31.360
There's a lot of discussion
these days about,

00:22:31.360 --> 00:22:34.100
are we running out of data
right on the internet?

00:22:34.100 --> 00:22:36.230
And so once you start
asking those questions,

00:22:36.230 --> 00:22:38.930
the other interesting and
important question is, well,

00:22:38.930 --> 00:22:41.510
can we just keep training
on the same data we have,

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:43.520
what's the diminishing
returns property of that?

00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:47.260
And so there's interesting
work extending scaling laws

00:22:47.260 --> 00:22:51.850
to multi-epoch training,
basically arguing that there's

00:22:51.850 --> 00:22:55.600
effective sample size, and
after about four epochs,

00:22:55.600 --> 00:22:57.490
you have rapidly
diminishing returns

00:22:57.490 --> 00:22:59.570
as you repeat more
and more data.

00:22:59.570 --> 00:23:03.290
And by modifying the
usual scaling law,

00:23:03.290 --> 00:23:06.070
you can basically
get a version where

00:23:06.070 --> 00:23:08.320
you have amount
of effective data

00:23:08.320 --> 00:23:11.770
and unique tokens that
diminish out as you increase

00:23:11.770 --> 00:23:15.340
the amount of repetition.

00:23:15.340 --> 00:23:19.420
Finally, I think one interesting
combination of these two ideas

00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:23.810
is if you're thinking about
data selection in the large data

00:23:23.810 --> 00:23:24.720
regime.

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:26.720
Imagine you're going to
be training on trillions

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:28.580
and trillions of tokens.

00:23:28.580 --> 00:23:30.420
Now, what would be better?

00:23:30.420 --> 00:23:32.300
Would it be better to
repeat high quality

00:23:32.300 --> 00:23:34.850
sources, like
Wikipedia and perhaps

00:23:34.850 --> 00:23:37.200
your secret pirated
books 10 times,

00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:39.510
or would it be better
to include new data?

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:42.080
The fact that you can
either repeat data

00:23:42.080 --> 00:23:46.700
or you can include more data now
has multiple axes on which you

00:23:46.700 --> 00:23:49.220
can optimize your data mixture.

00:23:49.220 --> 00:23:52.160
And there's also been some
interesting data scaling work--

00:23:52.160 --> 00:23:54.230
this one from CMU folks--

00:23:54.230 --> 00:23:56.420
on essentially trading
off between repeating

00:23:56.420 --> 00:24:01.100
data versus picking lower
quality data that's new.

00:24:01.100 --> 00:24:04.970
And so all of this really is
a really natural extension

00:24:04.970 --> 00:24:07.460
of what I already
taught you, which

00:24:07.460 --> 00:24:10.400
is if you assume that there's
a predictive power law

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:14.540
relationship and that this power
law relationship holds on a per

00:24:14.540 --> 00:24:18.500
mixture basis, then you can fit
these scaling law extrapolations

00:24:18.500 --> 00:24:20.630
and then get an estimate
of how good your data is

00:24:20.630 --> 00:24:22.000
going to be at scale.

00:24:24.530 --> 00:24:27.870
So that's the starting
point, which is data scaling.

00:24:27.870 --> 00:24:31.430
And hopefully I've convinced you
at this point, both empirically

00:24:31.430 --> 00:24:34.580
and conceptually, that
it's natural to have

00:24:34.580 --> 00:24:39.590
log log linear relationships
between data and error.

00:24:39.590 --> 00:24:43.230
This relationship seems to hold
very robustly across domains,

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:45.650
across different
kinds of models.

00:24:45.650 --> 00:24:49.010
And you can have a nice, clean
theoretical understanding

00:24:49.010 --> 00:24:51.530
of what is happening here.

00:24:51.530 --> 00:24:53.175
And once you do
this, you can use

00:24:53.175 --> 00:24:55.550
this for all sorts of purposes,
like picking optimal data

00:24:55.550 --> 00:24:57.890
mixtures or whatever else.

00:24:57.890 --> 00:24:58.490
OK.

00:24:58.490 --> 00:24:59.570
Yes.

00:24:59.570 --> 00:25:03.540
How is the model size picked
on the data scaling plots?

00:25:03.540 --> 00:25:08.210
Yeah, so as I was kind
of saying back in--

00:25:08.210 --> 00:25:13.370
well not this slide, but let's
see-- back in this slide,

00:25:13.370 --> 00:25:16.350
when we think about
the data size scaling,

00:25:16.350 --> 00:25:19.770
the model is always picked
to be really, really large.

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:23.300
So the data is not
saturating your model.

00:25:23.300 --> 00:25:27.810
And you want to avoid being in
this irreducible error regime.

00:25:27.810 --> 00:25:30.230
So the model is always
picked to be large enough

00:25:30.230 --> 00:25:31.790
that you're in the
power law region

00:25:31.790 --> 00:25:34.250
whenever you're
only varying data.

00:25:34.250 --> 00:25:37.470
So is it just one model
size for all of them,

00:25:37.470 --> 00:25:40.160
that one really, really
big model size or this each

00:25:40.160 --> 00:25:41.880
point like a
different size model?

00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:44.550
Yeah, for example, for
this plot in particular,

00:25:44.550 --> 00:25:46.320
it's for-- it's
one big model size.

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:48.180
When you're looking
at, for example,

00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:51.590
compute scaling on this axis,
then data and model scale

00:25:51.590 --> 00:25:56.330
jointly at some
preordained ratio.

00:25:56.330 --> 00:25:57.140
Cool.

00:25:57.140 --> 00:25:59.190
Any other questions?

00:25:59.190 --> 00:25:59.690
Good.

00:25:59.690 --> 00:26:00.360
OK.

00:26:00.360 --> 00:26:03.620
Excellent.

00:26:03.620 --> 00:26:05.780
All right.

00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:10.010
So now, I think we get
to move from data scaling

00:26:10.010 --> 00:26:14.780
to, in my opinion, slightly more
mysterious kinds of scaling.

00:26:14.780 --> 00:26:18.680
And we're going to talk
about model scaling next.

00:26:18.680 --> 00:26:21.410
And I think this is a
more practical engineering

00:26:21.410 --> 00:26:24.030
set of questions that we're
now going to try to answer.

00:26:24.030 --> 00:26:27.020
So you're in charge of
building and shipping

00:26:27.020 --> 00:26:28.710
a really large language model.

00:26:28.710 --> 00:26:31.310
And there's a lot of
interesting ideas out there.

00:26:31.310 --> 00:26:34.290
You could train the
latest state space model.

00:26:34.290 --> 00:26:35.610
You could train a transformer.

00:26:35.610 --> 00:26:36.630
You could use Adam.

00:26:36.630 --> 00:26:37.440
You could use SGD.

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:39.350
People invent all
sorts of new tricks.

00:26:39.350 --> 00:26:42.980
Which ones are worth scaling
up and which ones are not?

00:26:42.980 --> 00:26:46.662
You could also take your
limited compute resources

00:26:46.662 --> 00:26:48.120
and spend them on
different things.

00:26:48.120 --> 00:26:51.260
You can train models for longer,
or you train bigger models.

00:26:51.260 --> 00:26:54.740
For a given FLOP, you can
trade between these two.

00:26:54.740 --> 00:26:56.720
And you could also do
things go and collect

00:26:56.720 --> 00:26:58.140
more data versus get more GPUs.

00:26:58.140 --> 00:27:00.630
There's a lot of different
things that you can do.

00:27:00.630 --> 00:27:04.070
And scaling laws allow you to
have a pretty simple procedure

00:27:04.070 --> 00:27:06.230
to just answer all
of these questions.

00:27:06.230 --> 00:27:10.518
So I'll go through the classic
Kaplan scaling law paper.

00:27:10.518 --> 00:27:12.060
If you're interested
in these topics,

00:27:12.060 --> 00:27:13.185
I encourage you to read it.

00:27:13.185 --> 00:27:16.950
It's just kind of a gold mine of
all these kinds of observations.

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:19.870
Some of it is old,
but it's, I think,

00:27:19.870 --> 00:27:22.200
still unmatched in the
thoroughness of all

00:27:22.200 --> 00:27:24.690
of the things that it
really studied in a fairly

00:27:24.690 --> 00:27:26.610
nice, unified setting.

00:27:26.610 --> 00:27:30.900
So architecture wise, you
might start by asking like,

00:27:30.900 --> 00:27:33.430
transformers versus LSTMs,
which one is better?

00:27:33.430 --> 00:27:35.760
Well, the brute
force way might be

00:27:35.760 --> 00:27:39.220
to scale up LSTMs and
up to GPT three level.

00:27:39.220 --> 00:27:42.360
And then you can figure out
whether it's good or not.

00:27:42.360 --> 00:27:44.580
The scaling law way
is much simpler.

00:27:44.580 --> 00:27:47.100
You basically train a bunch
of LSTMs and transformers

00:27:47.100 --> 00:27:50.590
across many different compute
thresholds or compute levels,

00:27:50.590 --> 00:27:53.380
and then you see what
happens as you scale them up.

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:55.560
And I think the trends
here are fairly clear.

00:27:55.560 --> 00:27:58.600
No matter how many layers
you have on your LSTMs,

00:27:58.600 --> 00:28:02.070
there's a pretty big gap,
pretty big constant factor gap

00:28:02.070 --> 00:28:04.090
between transformers and LSTMs.

00:28:04.090 --> 00:28:05.920
And remember, this
is a log scale.

00:28:05.920 --> 00:28:06.960
So this is kind of
saying something

00:28:06.960 --> 00:28:09.377
like, I don't know what the
exact numbers are, but imagine

00:28:09.377 --> 00:28:12.220
this is like 15 times
less efficient, right?

00:28:12.220 --> 00:28:15.570
That no matter where you are
on this plot, the LSTM is let's

00:28:15.570 --> 00:28:18.270
say 15 times less compute
efficient than a transformer.

00:28:18.270 --> 00:28:20.960
So there's a constant
factor compute penalty

00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:25.280
to using LSTMs, at
least in this plot.

00:28:25.280 --> 00:28:27.500
You could zoom out
and say, well, there's

00:28:27.500 --> 00:28:28.860
a lot more architectures.

00:28:28.860 --> 00:28:33.410
Which ones are really
good and worth doing?

00:28:33.410 --> 00:28:36.620
And some of the classic
papers, this one is by E. Tay

00:28:36.620 --> 00:28:40.400
and others at Google have
done exactly this kind

00:28:40.400 --> 00:28:43.340
of scaling work, where they
took a bunch of architectures

00:28:43.340 --> 00:28:47.100
on the right here and they
basically scaled them up.

00:28:47.100 --> 00:28:48.870
So the x-axis is the
amount of compute.

00:28:48.870 --> 00:28:51.870
The red line is basically
each architecture.

00:28:51.870 --> 00:28:54.330
And the green line is
the transformer baseline.

00:28:54.330 --> 00:28:56.180
And they asked
like, oh, can any of

00:28:56.180 --> 00:29:00.830
these alternative
architectures match or outscale

00:29:00.830 --> 00:29:01.950
the transformer?

00:29:01.950 --> 00:29:04.890
And what do they end up?

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:06.350
Well, actually,
the only thing that

00:29:06.350 --> 00:29:08.060
seems really
strongly and reliably

00:29:08.060 --> 00:29:11.750
beat the transformer is gated
linear units and mixture

00:29:11.750 --> 00:29:12.250
of experts.

00:29:12.250 --> 00:29:15.050
And I want you to know it,
that's exactly the kind of stuff

00:29:15.050 --> 00:29:16.380
that people are doing today.

00:29:16.380 --> 00:29:18.020
And so this is kind
of the scaling law

00:29:18.020 --> 00:29:20.990
version of that same
idea of saying, how would

00:29:20.990 --> 00:29:23.090
you have come to the
conclusion that we should

00:29:23.090 --> 00:29:25.130
be doing switch
transformers and GLU

00:29:25.130 --> 00:29:27.150
and for example,
not the performer?

00:29:27.150 --> 00:29:29.780
And the scaling law
provides some clear evidence

00:29:29.780 --> 00:29:32.245
of why you might
want to do that.

00:29:34.850 --> 00:29:37.770
Optimizer choice, I think,
follows a similar thing.

00:29:37.770 --> 00:29:39.710
This one's from Hestness.

00:29:39.710 --> 00:29:41.420
They compare SGD and ADAM.

00:29:41.420 --> 00:29:44.780
They find very similar to before
this kind of constant factor

00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:47.850
gap, right, in compute, in
this case, data set size,

00:29:47.850 --> 00:29:51.590
but of course, that translates
to compute in the effectiveness

00:29:51.590 --> 00:29:53.840
of ADAM versus SGD.

00:29:53.840 --> 00:29:57.270
RHN in this case is
Recurrent Highway Nets.

00:29:57.270 --> 00:29:59.240
You can ignore the details here.

00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:02.220
You kind of see the point of
how you would do this analysis,

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:04.720
rather than the specific
results that are shown here.

00:30:07.880 --> 00:30:09.890
In the beginning, I
also said something

00:30:09.890 --> 00:30:13.360
like, oh, depth versus width,
what should the aspect ratios

00:30:13.360 --> 00:30:13.860
be?

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:15.568
That was one of the
hyperparameter topics

00:30:15.568 --> 00:30:16.670
we talked about.

00:30:16.670 --> 00:30:21.750
And we see similar analysis, but
in scaling law form from Kaplan.

00:30:21.750 --> 00:30:23.450
I think this one is
intriguing, to me

00:30:23.450 --> 00:30:26.780
at least, because we might
think that deeper layers get

00:30:26.780 --> 00:30:28.280
dramatically
better, that there's

00:30:28.280 --> 00:30:31.110
clear separation between
the number of layers.

00:30:31.110 --> 00:30:35.510
But we see at least here that
there's actually a lot of slop.

00:30:35.510 --> 00:30:39.110
One layer is really bad, but a
lot of the other layer choices

00:30:39.110 --> 00:30:41.210
remain pretty stable.

00:30:41.210 --> 00:30:43.760
And hopefully this is
reminiscent of that slide

00:30:43.760 --> 00:30:45.710
I showed back in the
architecture lecture

00:30:45.710 --> 00:30:48.650
where I said, well, the aspect
ratio, the ratio of width

00:30:48.650 --> 00:30:53.030
to depth, roughly
something like 4 to 16

00:30:53.030 --> 00:30:55.140
or something was a
pretty natural number,

00:30:55.140 --> 00:30:57.080
but there's a really
wide basin in which

00:30:57.080 --> 00:30:58.410
you're approximately optimal.

00:30:58.410 --> 00:31:02.030
And this scaling law
analysis also backs that up.

00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:05.240
One important subtlety that
I do want to point out,

00:31:05.240 --> 00:31:07.610
and this one bites people
every now and then,

00:31:07.610 --> 00:31:09.660
is that not all
parameters are equal.

00:31:09.660 --> 00:31:14.210
Often, you want to do
parameter scaling analyzes.

00:31:14.210 --> 00:31:18.410
But if you were to say, count
embedding parameters as part

00:31:18.410 --> 00:31:21.750
of your model, well, you get a
pretty different scaling law.

00:31:21.750 --> 00:31:23.480
You get this kind of
weird looking thing

00:31:23.480 --> 00:31:25.760
that slightly bends over here.

00:31:25.760 --> 00:31:28.530
Whereas if you only consider
the non-embedding parameter,

00:31:28.530 --> 00:31:31.730
you see that much cleaner
result that I showed you before.

00:31:31.730 --> 00:31:35.130
So embedding layer parameters
don't really behave the same.

00:31:35.130 --> 00:31:36.710
And they don't
show the same kinds

00:31:36.710 --> 00:31:42.410
of log linear scaling as
the non-embedding parameters

00:31:42.410 --> 00:31:44.660
when you account for them.

00:31:44.660 --> 00:31:47.270
And they're related work on
saying not all parameters are

00:31:47.270 --> 00:31:51.450
the same on recent papers on
scaling mixtures of experts,

00:31:51.450 --> 00:31:53.330
where they're also
trying to figure out,

00:31:53.330 --> 00:31:55.370
what does it mean to
be a parameter when

00:31:55.370 --> 00:31:57.480
you have such sparsely
activated parameters?

00:31:57.480 --> 00:31:59.000
And in those kinds
of papers, they

00:31:59.000 --> 00:32:01.880
try to derive essentially
things like equivalent number

00:32:01.880 --> 00:32:04.040
of dense parameters
in order to try

00:32:04.040 --> 00:32:07.910
to normalize the number of
parameters in MOU, right?

00:32:11.830 --> 00:32:14.840
I've showed you this plot
earlier in the hyperparameter

00:32:14.840 --> 00:32:16.790
selection, but hopefully
now actually you

00:32:16.790 --> 00:32:20.930
see the full context, not just
the original the hyperparameter

00:32:20.930 --> 00:32:23.120
choice question.

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:25.740
We know that in many cases--

00:32:25.740 --> 00:32:28.590
I'll go back, let's
say, to here--

00:32:28.590 --> 00:32:31.040
often what we'll see
is scaling law curves

00:32:31.040 --> 00:32:32.430
that look like the following.

00:32:32.430 --> 00:32:35.120
You'll often see that
the slope of the curves

00:32:35.120 --> 00:32:36.210
remain very similar.

00:32:36.210 --> 00:32:37.310
They're non-crossing.

00:32:37.310 --> 00:32:41.280
And that they're constant factor
offsets between these curves.

00:32:41.280 --> 00:32:43.160
And whenever this
is true, well you

00:32:43.160 --> 00:32:46.400
can then do is you can take
a slice at a particular level

00:32:46.400 --> 00:32:48.530
of compute or a particular
set of hyperparameters

00:32:48.530 --> 00:32:52.580
and analyze the hyperparameter
trade very carefully, assuming

00:32:52.580 --> 00:32:55.860
and be safe in scaling that up.

00:32:55.860 --> 00:32:58.740
And so when you go
to Kaplan's paper,

00:32:58.740 --> 00:33:01.940
you'll see exactly these
kinds of analyzes being done,

00:33:01.940 --> 00:33:04.080
especially, I think,
the center one,

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:07.160
the aspect ratio plot is
definitely worth looking at.

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:09.342
They're not just scaling
up and down models,

00:33:09.342 --> 00:33:11.050
they're actually taking
different slices.

00:33:11.050 --> 00:33:13.140
So different sized
models 50 million,

00:33:13.140 --> 00:33:16.140
270 million, 1.5
billion, and they're

00:33:16.140 --> 00:33:19.195
looking at how the aspect
ratio changes the loss.

00:33:19.195 --> 00:33:20.820
And they kind of see
that, oh, actually

00:33:20.820 --> 00:33:23.100
the shape of the curve,
not just the scaling

00:33:23.100 --> 00:33:25.780
slopes actually remain similar.

00:33:25.780 --> 00:33:28.950
And this means that I can pick
an aspect ratio between 10

00:33:28.950 --> 00:33:33.060
to 100 and anything in
between will work fine

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:34.840
at all of these
different scales.

00:33:34.840 --> 00:33:37.810
And so this is, I think,
important to think about.

00:33:37.810 --> 00:33:41.930
I think initially when you're
trained in deep learning model

00:33:41.930 --> 00:33:43.930
training, you think about
hyperparameter tuning,

00:33:43.930 --> 00:33:46.050
but you want to be scale
aware in how you're

00:33:46.050 --> 00:33:47.230
tuning your hyperparameters.

00:33:47.230 --> 00:33:49.147
And that's a really big
difference in mindset,

00:33:49.147 --> 00:33:52.230
I think, between the
scaling law style approach

00:33:52.230 --> 00:33:53.760
and maybe what
you've been trained

00:33:53.760 --> 00:33:56.430
or what you've known naturally
think about in terms of,

00:33:56.430 --> 00:33:59.370
oh, let's just tune these
models at a small scale.

00:33:59.370 --> 00:34:03.270
And so the same is being done
kind of for feedforward ratio

00:34:03.270 --> 00:34:04.890
and for attention
head dimension.

00:34:04.890 --> 00:34:07.270
You're varying various
aspects of scale,

00:34:07.270 --> 00:34:10.940
and you're trying to see whether
the minima remains similar.

00:34:15.300 --> 00:34:18.750
Another important thing.

00:34:18.750 --> 00:34:22.540
Next-- actually maybe not next
lecture, but next, next lecture,

00:34:22.540 --> 00:34:25.650
I'm going to talk about
practical case studies

00:34:25.650 --> 00:34:28.830
almost of how people
have scaled up models.

00:34:28.830 --> 00:34:32.489
And we'll actually see that
batch size and learning rate

00:34:32.489 --> 00:34:34.800
are actually two really
tricky things that you

00:34:34.800 --> 00:34:37.120
have to deal with carefully
when you scale models up.

00:34:37.120 --> 00:34:39.120
So when you scale
models up, you're

00:34:39.120 --> 00:34:42.239
going to have to maybe think
about the optimal learning

00:34:42.239 --> 00:34:44.037
rate will be different
across model scales.

00:34:44.037 --> 00:34:45.870
And if you're doing
that, then actually also

00:34:45.870 --> 00:34:48.760
maybe the optimal batch size
might end up varying as well,

00:34:48.760 --> 00:34:51.120
because those two
are often co linked.

00:34:51.120 --> 00:34:53.760
And so we need to think about
what the right way of scaling

00:34:53.760 --> 00:34:56.280
batch sizes and how batch
size interacts with scale

00:34:56.280 --> 00:34:57.280
and also learning rates.

00:34:57.280 --> 00:35:00.670
I'll talk about those for
the next couple of slides.

00:35:00.670 --> 00:35:03.960
So batch size from the systems
lecture, hopefully you remember,

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:06.310
it has diminishing returns
past a certain point.

00:35:06.310 --> 00:35:11.160
So up until a certain point,
so when the batch size is

00:35:11.160 --> 00:35:12.970
smaller than the noise scale--

00:35:12.970 --> 00:35:14.880
we're on the left
hand side here--

00:35:14.880 --> 00:35:18.060
increasing batch size is
almost equivalent to taking

00:35:18.060 --> 00:35:19.060
more gradient steps.

00:35:19.060 --> 00:35:22.510
So that's roughly saying,
if I double my batch size,

00:35:22.510 --> 00:35:25.225
it's as good as taking
two gradient steps.

00:35:25.225 --> 00:35:27.100
And that's a really,
really good place to be.

00:35:27.100 --> 00:35:29.430
Because now you've
got the system's power

00:35:29.430 --> 00:35:31.050
of being able to
parallelize across

00:35:31.050 --> 00:35:33.630
the batch, while
having the optimization

00:35:33.630 --> 00:35:35.560
efficiency of taking two steps.

00:35:35.560 --> 00:35:37.290
But past a certain
point, you're going

00:35:37.290 --> 00:35:39.150
to have ineffective scaling.

00:35:39.150 --> 00:35:42.550
Where now you're noise scale in
your batch size are the same,

00:35:42.550 --> 00:35:45.840
and the additional samples in
your batch that you're taking,

00:35:45.840 --> 00:35:48.640
they're not reducing
useful noise.

00:35:48.640 --> 00:35:52.510
It's getting dominated by the
curvature of the bias term,

00:35:52.510 --> 00:35:57.420
so to speak, of the curvature
of your optimization landscape.

00:35:57.420 --> 00:36:00.130
And one really useful
thing to think about,

00:36:00.130 --> 00:36:05.110
useful analysis object is this
notion of a critical batch size.

00:36:05.110 --> 00:36:07.260
And the critical batch
size you can think of

00:36:07.260 --> 00:36:10.530
is this threshold point where
we go from perfect scaling

00:36:10.530 --> 00:36:13.090
to strong diminishing returns.

00:36:13.090 --> 00:36:17.430
And you can analyze this
in theory in OpenAI papers

00:36:17.430 --> 00:36:19.630
on critical batch sizes do this.

00:36:19.630 --> 00:36:23.080
But you could also
analyze this empirically.

00:36:23.080 --> 00:36:27.180
And this is another thing that's
been studied in this scaling law

00:36:27.180 --> 00:36:28.710
kind of way.

00:36:28.710 --> 00:36:34.540
You can see you can estimate the
point at which progress slows.

00:36:34.540 --> 00:36:37.050
So you can estimate empirically
what the critical batch size

00:36:37.050 --> 00:36:38.770
point trade off points are.

00:36:38.770 --> 00:36:43.120
And you can also basically
train bigger and better models.

00:36:43.120 --> 00:36:47.010
And one really interesting
thing is as you try to improve

00:36:47.010 --> 00:36:48.760
the loss-- so you're
going left side here,

00:36:48.760 --> 00:36:51.385
so you're making losses better
and better and better and better

00:36:51.385 --> 00:36:52.380
and better--

00:36:52.380 --> 00:36:55.630
your critical batch size ends
up getting smaller, right?

00:36:55.630 --> 00:36:59.460
So the smaller the loss target,
the bigger the overall batch

00:36:59.460 --> 00:37:02.940
size that you can be.

00:37:02.940 --> 00:37:05.070
And so one of the things
that this leads to

00:37:05.070 --> 00:37:08.980
is, for example, if you look at
the Llama three training report,

00:37:08.980 --> 00:37:10.602
you'll actually
see, for example,

00:37:10.602 --> 00:37:13.060
that they'll increase the batch
size after a certain point,

00:37:13.060 --> 00:37:14.477
or they'll do
things like increase

00:37:14.477 --> 00:37:17.370
the batch size as they train,
because as your loss target gets

00:37:17.370 --> 00:37:23.790
smaller, your batch sizes
can in turn get bigger.

00:37:23.790 --> 00:37:27.220
So as we increase both
compute and model size,

00:37:27.220 --> 00:37:28.690
what's the right thing to do?

00:37:28.690 --> 00:37:30.940
Once again, we can do
a scaling analysis.

00:37:30.940 --> 00:37:32.490
This is from Kaplan.

00:37:32.490 --> 00:37:34.980
And you can try to
figure out, as we

00:37:34.980 --> 00:37:39.670
increase the amount of compute,
what is the optimal batch size?

00:37:39.670 --> 00:37:44.760
And when we see is that, as we
increase the amount of compute,

00:37:44.760 --> 00:37:48.370
we can actually have
reasonable parallelism.

00:37:48.370 --> 00:37:50.915
The number of total
steps can stay the same,

00:37:50.915 --> 00:37:52.540
at least within this
compute threshold,

00:37:52.540 --> 00:37:55.650
the number of total steps
can stay the same while

00:37:55.650 --> 00:37:57.770
getting the batches bigger
and bigger and bigger.

00:37:57.770 --> 00:37:59.770
And if you fix the amount
of batches, of course,

00:37:59.770 --> 00:38:01.937
the number of steps is going
to go up and up and up.

00:38:01.937 --> 00:38:05.790
So this is good news hopefully
for data parallel processing.

00:38:05.790 --> 00:38:07.570
So that's the batch size story.

00:38:07.570 --> 00:38:09.510
The thing that you
should maybe remember

00:38:09.510 --> 00:38:11.520
because I think critical
batch sizes are kind

00:38:11.520 --> 00:38:16.020
of a messy concept is that, a,
there's a diminishing returns

00:38:16.020 --> 00:38:17.340
point, the critical batch size.

00:38:17.340 --> 00:38:18.250
That's one thing.

00:38:18.250 --> 00:38:19.770
The second one is
that it does seem

00:38:19.770 --> 00:38:21.610
to follow a pretty
predictable scaling,

00:38:21.610 --> 00:38:24.150
often as a function
of your target loss,

00:38:24.150 --> 00:38:26.250
and given that
you can figure out

00:38:26.250 --> 00:38:28.170
what is the right
trade-offs that I can

00:38:28.170 --> 00:38:31.920
make in terms of systems
efficiency and my optimization

00:38:31.920 --> 00:38:34.710
progress.

00:38:34.710 --> 00:38:38.745
As I said before, the
other aspect of this

00:38:38.745 --> 00:38:41.172
is you've got your batch
size and then you've

00:38:41.172 --> 00:38:42.130
got your learning rate.

00:38:42.130 --> 00:38:45.420
And those two are fairly
closely linked with each other.

00:38:45.420 --> 00:38:49.170
And I'm going to talk about mu
P at much more extensive length

00:38:49.170 --> 00:38:51.840
in the next part of
the scaling lecture.

00:38:51.840 --> 00:38:55.653
But this is a really important,
I think, broader idea.

00:38:55.653 --> 00:38:57.070
So you could do
one of two things.

00:38:57.070 --> 00:38:59.570
And I this figure will allow
me to talk about both of these.

00:38:59.570 --> 00:39:02.100
So let's look at
this left plot first,

00:39:02.100 --> 00:39:04.090
what's labeled
standard practice.

00:39:04.090 --> 00:39:06.813
So when you train a transformer,
right, what you're basically

00:39:06.813 --> 00:39:08.730
going to see is something
like this left thing

00:39:08.730 --> 00:39:11.190
here, this standard practice.

00:39:11.190 --> 00:39:14.850
So the optimal learning rate is
going to be at different points.

00:39:14.850 --> 00:39:18.060
And the wider the model, right,
as you increase your model size

00:39:18.060 --> 00:39:20.680
and your MLPs get wider
and wider and wider,

00:39:20.680 --> 00:39:23.500
the optimal learning rate
is going to be pretty small.

00:39:23.500 --> 00:39:26.225
And as you make your model
smaller and smaller and smaller,

00:39:26.225 --> 00:39:27.600
your losses, of
course, are going

00:39:27.600 --> 00:39:30.580
to go up because your
model is less expressive,

00:39:30.580 --> 00:39:34.912
but also the optimum learning
rate is going to also go up.

00:39:34.912 --> 00:39:37.870
And often, people say
there's a rule of thumb.

00:39:37.870 --> 00:39:40.440
It's 1 over the width is
the right rate at which you

00:39:40.440 --> 00:39:42.570
should scale the learning rate.

00:39:42.570 --> 00:39:45.330
More advanced people
will actually fit.

00:39:45.330 --> 00:39:47.980
Basically take these
curves, find the minimum,

00:39:47.980 --> 00:39:50.735
and then fit a scaling law
on the optimum learning rate.

00:39:50.735 --> 00:39:52.110
And so there we
can see that this

00:39:52.110 --> 00:39:53.962
is a predictable
decay in learning rate

00:39:53.962 --> 00:39:55.420
and maybe we can
fit a scaling law.

00:39:55.420 --> 00:39:58.800
I'll talk about this more
in the next set of lectures.

00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:01.140
But an alternative
one that I think

00:40:01.140 --> 00:40:02.910
many people have
started to adopt

00:40:02.910 --> 00:40:05.700
and I think is a really
interesting thing to think about

00:40:05.700 --> 00:40:09.810
is that you can actually
reparametrize the model.

00:40:09.810 --> 00:40:11.730
And in particular,
you can do things

00:40:11.730 --> 00:40:14.670
like scale the
initialization-- or scale

00:40:14.670 --> 00:40:18.490
the learning rates of different
layers based on the width.

00:40:18.490 --> 00:40:21.540
You can scale the variance
of the initialization

00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:24.450
based on the width of
the model, as well as

00:40:24.450 --> 00:40:27.810
multiply the output in the
forward pass of different layers

00:40:27.810 --> 00:40:29.670
of the model.

00:40:29.670 --> 00:40:32.520
And if you do this
in a way that is

00:40:32.520 --> 00:40:35.490
dependent on the
width of the model,

00:40:35.490 --> 00:40:37.950
you end up with a
parameterization

00:40:37.950 --> 00:40:41.220
of the model whose
learning rate is supposed

00:40:41.220 --> 00:40:44.080
to be more stable, or at
least in the original paper,

00:40:44.080 --> 00:40:46.500
exactly stable across scale.

00:40:46.500 --> 00:40:48.300
So you tune your
learning rate once

00:40:48.300 --> 00:40:50.310
and you don't have to
do anything else that

00:40:50.310 --> 00:40:51.677
optimum directly transfer.

00:40:51.677 --> 00:40:53.760
It's actually you tune it
here on the smallest one

00:40:53.760 --> 00:40:57.240
and that directly transfers
to the very largest scale.

00:40:57.240 --> 00:41:01.750
And this is the
idea called mu P.

00:41:01.750 --> 00:41:03.420
this original paper
that I'm showing you

00:41:03.420 --> 00:41:06.390
is called width mu P.
There's been other variants.

00:41:06.390 --> 00:41:08.580
Meta with the release
of Llama 4 claims

00:41:08.580 --> 00:41:11.820
to have invented something
called MetaP, which I'm not

00:41:11.820 --> 00:41:13.560
quite sure what it is yet.

00:41:13.560 --> 00:41:15.840
But you can see
that a lot of labs

00:41:15.840 --> 00:41:17.430
are kind of thinking about this.

00:41:17.430 --> 00:41:20.670
Because if you're going to
have to rely on predicting what

00:41:20.670 --> 00:41:22.500
the optimum learning
rate is, then you

00:41:22.500 --> 00:41:24.640
have to do all sorts of
tricky scaling law fits.

00:41:24.640 --> 00:41:27.630
And maybe this is very unstable,
but if you can reparameterize

00:41:27.630 --> 00:41:29.370
your model, then,
well, maybe you

00:41:29.370 --> 00:41:31.870
don't have to do any
re-tuning at all.

00:41:31.870 --> 00:41:33.960
Of course, that's way
more optimistic than what

00:41:33.960 --> 00:41:35.910
happens in practice,
but hopefully this

00:41:35.910 --> 00:41:39.180
gives you a sense of why this
is really cool and really

00:41:39.180 --> 00:41:39.870
interesting.

00:41:39.870 --> 00:41:43.800
Scale aware initializations.

00:41:43.800 --> 00:41:44.680
Cool.

00:41:44.680 --> 00:41:46.150
Any questions up
until this point?

00:41:46.150 --> 00:41:49.830
I feel like I've gone through
a whole bunch of scaling

00:41:49.830 --> 00:41:51.610
architecture and
parameters stuff,

00:41:51.610 --> 00:41:54.660
so maybe I'll stop for a
moment here in case anyone

00:41:54.660 --> 00:41:55.990
has any questions.

00:41:55.990 --> 00:41:57.240
Yeah.

00:41:57.240 --> 00:41:59.130
I really get the
intuition behind

00:41:59.130 --> 00:42:01.575
like if we want to
lower loss target

00:42:01.575 --> 00:42:03.840
and we want to increase
the batch size.

00:42:03.840 --> 00:42:06.120
I didn't really I
understand that.

00:42:06.120 --> 00:42:06.960
Yeah.

00:42:06.960 --> 00:42:11.940
So when you have a
lower loss target,

00:42:11.940 --> 00:42:18.300
yeah, so what you want
to do is the smaller

00:42:18.300 --> 00:42:20.830
the loss target, the more
sensitive things are.

00:42:20.830 --> 00:42:22.380
And in the same way
that you're going

00:42:22.380 --> 00:42:24.540
to be lowering
your learning rate,

00:42:24.540 --> 00:42:27.910
you want to also increase your
batch size in order to denoise.

00:42:27.910 --> 00:42:31.860
The more sensitive the target
that you have, the more precise

00:42:31.860 --> 00:42:33.810
your gradients
potentially have to be.

00:42:33.810 --> 00:42:36.810
One way of thinking about it
is like as you're cooling down

00:42:36.810 --> 00:42:38.358
in your learning
rate is going up,

00:42:38.358 --> 00:42:39.900
maybe your batch
size should increase

00:42:39.900 --> 00:42:42.358
as well, because the learning
rate and batch size is-- sort

00:42:42.358 --> 00:42:44.790
of affect each other inversely.

00:42:44.790 --> 00:42:45.990
Yeah.

00:42:45.990 --> 00:42:51.180
So the batch size thing is only
true for MLP, not true for MPV?

00:42:51.180 --> 00:42:52.760
Or for computer vision?

00:42:52.760 --> 00:42:54.260
I'm not sure.

00:42:54.260 --> 00:42:58.490
There is a related
OpenAI scaling

00:42:58.490 --> 00:43:00.613
paper for multimodal
models, but I'm not--

00:43:00.613 --> 00:43:02.780
I don't remember what that
says about critical batch

00:43:02.780 --> 00:43:03.810
size for those.

00:43:03.810 --> 00:43:05.600
Yeah.

00:43:05.600 --> 00:43:06.410
Yes.

00:43:06.410 --> 00:43:08.390
What's noise scale?

00:43:08.390 --> 00:43:09.360
The noise scale?

00:43:09.360 --> 00:43:10.220
Yeah.

00:43:10.220 --> 00:43:12.312
The noise scale, at
least in this figure,

00:43:12.312 --> 00:43:13.770
if that's what
you're asking about,

00:43:13.770 --> 00:43:16.095
this is a kind of
theoretical analysis.

00:43:16.095 --> 00:43:17.720
It's basically about
the gradient noise

00:43:17.720 --> 00:43:20.880
that you expect from random
sampling within the batch.

00:43:20.880 --> 00:43:25.212
So this is not like a precisely
empirically measured quantity,

00:43:25.212 --> 00:43:25.920
at least in this.

00:43:28.580 --> 00:43:29.150
Cool.

00:43:29.150 --> 00:43:29.650
OK.

00:43:33.260 --> 00:43:34.140
All right.

00:43:34.140 --> 00:43:36.950
So one thing I
will caution, and I

00:43:36.950 --> 00:43:39.930
think this is a big caution
for a lot of scaling law works,

00:43:39.930 --> 00:43:44.040
is that scaling laws are very
nicely behaved for log losses.

00:43:44.040 --> 00:43:48.410
So we train on next token
prediction cross-entropy.

00:43:48.410 --> 00:43:52.120
When you're scaling law targets
are those cross entropies,

00:43:52.120 --> 00:43:54.790
the very easy works very well.

00:43:54.790 --> 00:43:58.100
But if you're trying to
do downstream tasks right,

00:43:58.100 --> 00:44:00.470
you're trying to directly
scale on benchmarks,

00:44:00.470 --> 00:44:02.450
behavior is much
less predictable.

00:44:02.450 --> 00:44:04.460
So here on the left side--

00:44:04.460 --> 00:44:07.420
this is from E.
Tay's paper comparing

00:44:07.420 --> 00:44:09.190
lots of different
hyperparameters

00:44:09.190 --> 00:44:12.490
and architectures-- you see that
the number of parameters, which

00:44:12.490 --> 00:44:14.290
in this case is a
surrogate for compute,

00:44:14.290 --> 00:44:18.010
and the negative log
perplexity is very nicely

00:44:18.010 --> 00:44:19.148
linearly correlated.

00:44:19.148 --> 00:44:20.690
And what this is
basically saying is,

00:44:20.690 --> 00:44:22.990
well, it doesn't
matter what your depth

00:44:22.990 --> 00:44:26.403
or width or precise setting
of the hyperparameters are.

00:44:26.403 --> 00:44:27.820
The only thing
that really matters

00:44:27.820 --> 00:44:29.560
is your total
compute expenditure.

00:44:29.560 --> 00:44:31.820
This is a very simple
and nice story.

00:44:31.820 --> 00:44:33.620
But then you take these models.

00:44:33.620 --> 00:44:35.180
This was back in 2023.

00:44:35.180 --> 00:44:39.250
So people were still kind
of doing superglue accuracy.

00:44:39.250 --> 00:44:41.800
And you basically
say, OK, but what's

00:44:41.800 --> 00:44:43.730
the downstream performance
of these models?

00:44:43.730 --> 00:44:46.810
And well, now we don't see a
very nice linear relationship

00:44:46.810 --> 00:44:47.360
anymore.

00:44:47.360 --> 00:44:49.510
We see this totally
different thing

00:44:49.510 --> 00:44:51.010
where certain models
are much better

00:44:51.010 --> 00:44:52.660
than others and
certain architectures

00:44:52.660 --> 00:44:54.430
are better than others.

00:44:54.430 --> 00:44:56.890
And so you might not
expect exactly this kind

00:44:56.890 --> 00:44:58.600
of scaling property.

00:44:58.600 --> 00:45:00.970
And we've seen
variants of this story

00:45:00.970 --> 00:45:03.040
play out in many
different places.

00:45:03.040 --> 00:45:06.070
If you follow the literature
on state space models,

00:45:06.070 --> 00:45:08.410
that's one thing
that we've seen.

00:45:08.410 --> 00:45:12.340
In state space models, we see
really nice, predictable scaling

00:45:12.340 --> 00:45:16.090
like the ones on the left, but
often for certain capabilities.

00:45:16.090 --> 00:45:19.060
Like in-context
learning or for QA,

00:45:19.060 --> 00:45:22.130
people have shown that these
models maybe do less well.

00:45:22.130 --> 00:45:26.560
So it's important to not
take this perplexity scaling

00:45:26.560 --> 00:45:28.397
as the same thing as
downstream scaling.

00:45:28.397 --> 00:45:30.730
And you want to be a little
bit cautious whenever you're

00:45:30.730 --> 00:45:34.810
doing these kinds of analyzes.

00:45:34.810 --> 00:45:39.890
OK, so maybe this is not
surprising to some of you,

00:45:39.890 --> 00:45:44.410
but hopefully this is
surprising and convincing,

00:45:44.410 --> 00:45:47.770
which is that if we want to make
lots of engineering decisions,

00:45:47.770 --> 00:45:50.920
like hyperparameter choices,
architecture decisions,

00:45:50.920 --> 00:45:52.960
we can do a lot of
that before training.

00:45:52.960 --> 00:45:55.000
We can train these
models at small scale

00:45:55.000 --> 00:45:57.530
across several orders
of magnitude, compute.

00:45:57.530 --> 00:45:59.500
And then you scale
that up in order

00:45:59.500 --> 00:46:02.090
to try to predict the
behavior of models.

00:46:02.090 --> 00:46:05.060
So the scaling law based design
procedure is pretty simple.

00:46:05.060 --> 00:46:07.540
You train a few smaller models,
and these smaller models

00:46:07.540 --> 00:46:09.790
should span a couple orders
of magnitude compute.

00:46:09.790 --> 00:46:12.170
You establish a scaling
law of some kind.

00:46:12.170 --> 00:46:15.020
So you see that at least on
the models that you trained,

00:46:15.020 --> 00:46:17.990
that there is a clear log
log linear relationship.

00:46:17.990 --> 00:46:19.780
And then based on
this prediction,

00:46:19.780 --> 00:46:22.240
you can set optimal
hyperparameters.

00:46:22.240 --> 00:46:24.820
In many cases, in fact,
these scaling laws

00:46:24.820 --> 00:46:26.085
won't really vary too much.

00:46:26.085 --> 00:46:27.710
Their slopes will
actually be the same.

00:46:27.710 --> 00:46:30.070
In which case, the
corollary to this is

00:46:30.070 --> 00:46:32.030
can just train a
few smaller models

00:46:32.030 --> 00:46:34.120
and the results of
those small models

00:46:34.120 --> 00:46:37.750
will transfer surprisingly
well to the larger models

00:46:37.750 --> 00:46:40.240
in many of these cases, but
not all of them, learning rate

00:46:40.240 --> 00:46:43.300
being an important
exception, for example.

00:46:43.300 --> 00:46:46.960
OK, so that's how you do things
like hyperparameter selection

00:46:46.960 --> 00:46:48.520
and architecture selection.

00:46:48.520 --> 00:46:51.640
Now, I want to talk about one
very important use of scaling

00:46:51.640 --> 00:46:56.290
laws, one that's had an outsized
influence on how we pick sizes

00:46:56.290 --> 00:46:59.110
of models, how we think
about data efficiency

00:46:59.110 --> 00:47:00.680
and so on of these models.

00:47:00.680 --> 00:47:04.360
So I think back in
the earlier days

00:47:04.360 --> 00:47:07.220
when people were beginning
to scale up these models,

00:47:07.220 --> 00:47:09.580
there's a really core
question that you need to ask.

00:47:09.580 --> 00:47:12.530
Do we need more data, or
do we need bigger models?

00:47:12.530 --> 00:47:18.850
In some sense, back in
2021 to 2023 or something,

00:47:18.850 --> 00:47:20.750
data was way more
abundant than compute.

00:47:20.750 --> 00:47:25.070
So we didn't need to worry about
the total data limitations.

00:47:25.070 --> 00:47:27.673
And so the one limiting
resource is compute.

00:47:27.673 --> 00:47:29.840
Your total number of FLOPs
for your training budget,

00:47:29.840 --> 00:47:31.490
that's kind of the
limiting resource.

00:47:31.490 --> 00:47:34.400
And you can then spend that
resource in many different ways.

00:47:34.400 --> 00:47:37.490
You can spend it on training on
lots of data with a small model,

00:47:37.490 --> 00:47:41.300
or you can train one giant
model on very little data.

00:47:41.300 --> 00:47:43.930
And both of those extremes
seem very wasteful.

00:47:43.930 --> 00:47:47.440
If you have a teeny tiny model,
pumping in tons and tons of data

00:47:47.440 --> 00:47:49.660
doesn't seem useful and reverse.

00:47:49.660 --> 00:47:51.430
If you have a giant
model with 10 tokens,

00:47:51.430 --> 00:47:54.160
it also doesn't
seem very useful.

00:47:54.160 --> 00:47:57.910
And so this was a core
question for many people.

00:47:57.910 --> 00:48:01.000
And so there simultaneously
several authors

00:48:01.000 --> 00:48:04.870
proposed joint data
model scaling laws

00:48:04.870 --> 00:48:06.513
to try to answer this question.

00:48:06.513 --> 00:48:07.430
And so what are those?

00:48:07.430 --> 00:48:10.930
I've been talking about
scaling laws in essentially one

00:48:10.930 --> 00:48:13.435
variable exclusively
up until this point.

00:48:13.435 --> 00:48:14.810
And that one
variable has varied.

00:48:14.810 --> 00:48:17.830
It has sometimes been
parameters or data or compute.

00:48:17.830 --> 00:48:20.680
But we've not looked at
joint scaling, right?

00:48:20.680 --> 00:48:24.320
And so data model scaling laws
are things that look like this.

00:48:24.320 --> 00:48:26.860
These two equations
here are both

00:48:26.860 --> 00:48:31.030
like functionally equivalent
to first order and describe

00:48:31.030 --> 00:48:32.800
the trade off between
the amount of data

00:48:32.800 --> 00:48:34.660
and the amount of models.

00:48:34.660 --> 00:48:37.540
So the top one from
Rosenfeld is basically

00:48:37.540 --> 00:48:40.690
saying there's a part of the
error, one part of it that

00:48:40.690 --> 00:48:42.530
decays polynomially in data.

00:48:42.530 --> 00:48:44.890
There's a part of the error
that decays polynomially

00:48:44.890 --> 00:48:46.340
in the model size.

00:48:46.340 --> 00:48:48.820
And then there's an irreducible
error term that cannot be

00:48:48.820 --> 00:48:52.120
removed, even if I scale both
the data size and the model

00:48:52.120 --> 00:48:52.840
to infinity.

00:48:52.840 --> 00:48:54.800
Same effect with Kaplan.

00:48:54.800 --> 00:48:58.750
But here, they're thinking about
irreducible error rather than

00:48:58.750 --> 00:48:59.870
reducible error.

00:48:59.870 --> 00:49:02.830
And so there's no
constant term here.

00:49:02.830 --> 00:49:05.530
So this seems kind of
arbitrary because I

00:49:05.530 --> 00:49:10.540
don't think there's any top,
down reason why this has to be

00:49:10.540 --> 00:49:11.780
the correct functional form.

00:49:11.780 --> 00:49:14.980
But this provides surprisingly
good fits to the joint error

00:49:14.980 --> 00:49:17.180
that you see in data and models.

00:49:17.180 --> 00:49:19.400
So this is from, I
believe, Rosenfeld.

00:49:19.400 --> 00:49:24.490
They show this nice 3D plot
of this is the amount of data.

00:49:24.490 --> 00:49:25.940
This is the size of the model.

00:49:25.940 --> 00:49:28.100
And this is the
loss on the y-axis.

00:49:28.100 --> 00:49:30.590
And this surface that's being
fit is their functional form.

00:49:30.590 --> 00:49:32.382
The dots are their runs.

00:49:32.382 --> 00:49:34.340
It might be a little hard
to see from the back,

00:49:34.340 --> 00:49:36.813
but the surface fits
the dots almost exactly.

00:49:39.490 --> 00:49:42.490
And despite the fact
that this functional form

00:49:42.490 --> 00:49:45.100
is kind of ad hoc, like
it's pulled out of a hat,

00:49:45.100 --> 00:49:47.140
it is surprisingly accurate.

00:49:47.140 --> 00:49:51.200
This one's from Rosenfeld as
well, where they basically say,

00:49:51.200 --> 00:49:55.555
OK, I'm only going to train
on essentially the small half,

00:49:55.555 --> 00:49:58.480
models that are small
and data that is small so

00:49:58.480 --> 00:50:00.280
on this left bottom,
and I'm going

00:50:00.280 --> 00:50:04.000
to extrapolate to models
that are of both large

00:50:04.000 --> 00:50:05.780
and trained with more models.

00:50:05.780 --> 00:50:08.510
And how good is that fit
of joint extrapolation?

00:50:08.510 --> 00:50:10.160
Well, quite good right.

00:50:10.160 --> 00:50:16.850
So if you look at the error, my
real values are on the x-axis.

00:50:16.850 --> 00:50:19.640
My predictions of the
error on the y-axis.

00:50:19.640 --> 00:50:22.570
And they're almost exactly
right, both on ImageNet

00:50:22.570 --> 00:50:25.210
and on Wikitext.

00:50:25.210 --> 00:50:28.150
So this seems pretty good.

00:50:28.150 --> 00:50:32.750
And so for a fixed compute
budget, now what can we do?

00:50:32.750 --> 00:50:34.670
We go back to, for
example, Kaplan

00:50:34.670 --> 00:50:36.800
and we see similar
things being done here.

00:50:36.800 --> 00:50:40.340
We see joint scaling
of compute and data.

00:50:40.340 --> 00:50:43.160
So in this case, parameters
are on the x-axis.

00:50:43.160 --> 00:50:45.290
The colors represent compute.

00:50:45.290 --> 00:50:47.770
And so there's a
third axis of data

00:50:47.770 --> 00:50:50.273
that's being implicitly
varied in order to vary

00:50:50.273 --> 00:50:51.440
the total amount of compute.

00:50:51.440 --> 00:50:54.910
So as you go shift
in on these curves,

00:50:54.910 --> 00:50:58.270
the parameters are being
varied while the compute is

00:50:58.270 --> 00:51:01.200
being held constant, and so the
amount of data is going to vary.

00:51:04.900 --> 00:51:08.950
So Chinchilla, I think many of
you have hopefully heard of,

00:51:08.950 --> 00:51:12.350
is probably the reference
in solving this problem.

00:51:12.350 --> 00:51:16.960
So both Rosenfeld and Kaplan
came up with this joint scaling

00:51:16.960 --> 00:51:18.110
functional form.

00:51:18.110 --> 00:51:21.160
And then both of
them noticed that it

00:51:21.160 --> 00:51:24.280
was possible to use these
functional forms to optimize

00:51:24.280 --> 00:51:28.540
the trade off between compute
and data in various ways.

00:51:28.540 --> 00:51:31.720
But for various
reasons, basically it's

00:51:31.720 --> 00:51:34.125
hard to fit these
functional forms precisely

00:51:34.125 --> 00:51:36.940
and the details like
the learning rate

00:51:36.940 --> 00:51:40.440
shapes being different
are important.

00:51:40.440 --> 00:51:43.730
And so Kaplan had
one estimate that

00:51:43.730 --> 00:51:47.270
was quite far off from what was
later, in some sense, validated

00:51:47.270 --> 00:51:48.570
to be optimal.

00:51:48.570 --> 00:51:52.595
And so the Chinchilla paper
by a bunch of Google authors

00:51:52.595 --> 00:51:54.290
was an attempt to
really empirically try

00:51:54.290 --> 00:51:57.020
to nail down what is
the right trade off

00:51:57.020 --> 00:52:00.300
between the amount of
tokens and the model size,

00:52:00.300 --> 00:52:02.120
assuming that your
goal is to get

00:52:02.120 --> 00:52:05.883
the best model for the smallest
amount of training FLOPs.

00:52:05.883 --> 00:52:07.550
So they have three
different approaches,

00:52:07.550 --> 00:52:10.250
approach 1, 2, and
3 for basically

00:52:10.250 --> 00:52:12.930
fitting different curves and
making scaling predictions.

00:52:12.930 --> 00:52:15.770
These blue dots are the
models that they trained.

00:52:15.770 --> 00:52:18.140
And basically, the
lines are predicting

00:52:18.140 --> 00:52:21.390
different optimal parameter
sizes for different FLOPs.

00:52:21.390 --> 00:52:24.750
And hopefully most of you
know the Chinchilla ratio.

00:52:24.750 --> 00:52:27.660
That's something like
20 tokens per parameter.

00:52:27.660 --> 00:52:29.400
And that comes
from exactly this.

00:52:29.400 --> 00:52:32.490
If you take each of these points
and you multiply it by 20,

00:52:32.490 --> 00:52:35.273
you're going to get roughly the
FLOP or-- sorry-- multiplied

00:52:35.273 --> 00:52:36.690
by 20, you'll get
the token count.

00:52:36.690 --> 00:52:39.030
And so if you multiply
the parameters by that,

00:52:39.030 --> 00:52:40.135
you'll get the FLOPs.

00:52:43.220 --> 00:52:46.380
The difference between
the Kaplan results,

00:52:46.380 --> 00:52:48.710
which were basically
estimating one

00:52:48.710 --> 00:52:52.980
set of token to parameter
ratios, and the Chinchilla ones.

00:52:52.980 --> 00:52:56.870
One of the reasons is because
of learning rate schedules.

00:52:56.870 --> 00:53:00.240
We know that we train models
with cosine learning rates.

00:53:00.240 --> 00:53:01.683
So cosine learning
rates are going

00:53:01.683 --> 00:53:02.850
to look something like this.

00:53:02.850 --> 00:53:04.577
It goes up, and then
it comes back down.

00:53:04.577 --> 00:53:06.410
And then it's going to
cool down all the way

00:53:06.410 --> 00:53:08.410
to a minimum learning
rate at your bottom.

00:53:10.988 --> 00:53:12.530
One thing about
cosine learning rates

00:53:12.530 --> 00:53:14.390
that trips everyone
up all the time

00:53:14.390 --> 00:53:16.550
is you can't
truncate them early.

00:53:16.550 --> 00:53:18.440
For cosine learning
rate, you have

00:53:18.440 --> 00:53:21.770
to go all the way to the end
in order to get a valid model.

00:53:21.770 --> 00:53:24.330
You have to get a cool down
phase all the way to the end.

00:53:24.330 --> 00:53:26.130
If I truncate a
model in the middle,

00:53:26.130 --> 00:53:29.060
this is not the same as
starting a model from scratch

00:53:29.060 --> 00:53:31.370
and training it with a cosine
learning rate somewhere

00:53:31.370 --> 00:53:32.510
in the middle.

00:53:32.510 --> 00:53:35.220
And this was one of the
contributing factors--

00:53:35.220 --> 00:53:36.990
there were others as well--

00:53:36.990 --> 00:53:39.660
leading to the Kaplan
estimates being

00:53:39.660 --> 00:53:43.560
pretty far off from the
later more improved estimates

00:53:43.560 --> 00:53:48.120
provided by the
Chinchilla paper.

00:53:48.120 --> 00:53:51.490
So what do the Chinchilla
authors actually do?

00:53:51.490 --> 00:53:53.190
Well, they have three
different methods

00:53:53.190 --> 00:53:56.430
of trying to estimate
the optimum trade off

00:53:56.430 --> 00:53:58.270
between tokens to models.

00:53:58.270 --> 00:54:00.360
And each of these
methods are going

00:54:00.360 --> 00:54:03.360
to provide different scaling
coefficients, scaling

00:54:03.360 --> 00:54:06.780
coefficients for the model
size and scaling coefficients

00:54:06.780 --> 00:54:08.760
for the data size.

00:54:08.760 --> 00:54:10.780
And kind of surprisingly,
in this case,

00:54:10.780 --> 00:54:14.620
they're getting 0.5 on both of
these for methods one and two.

00:54:14.620 --> 00:54:18.030
And method three is providing
pretty different or slightly

00:54:18.030 --> 00:54:18.970
different estimates.

00:54:18.970 --> 00:54:21.240
There are about off by 0.03.

00:54:21.240 --> 00:54:23.470
But we'll talk about
that a little bit later.

00:54:23.470 --> 00:54:26.640
Kaplan et al, you
see, is way off

00:54:26.640 --> 00:54:29.345
than any of the three estimates.

00:54:29.345 --> 00:54:30.970
So we'll go over each
of these methods.

00:54:30.970 --> 00:54:32.770
Each of these makes sense.

00:54:32.770 --> 00:54:35.210
They make different
assumptions about scaling,

00:54:35.210 --> 00:54:38.330
but they end up with very,
very similar estimates

00:54:38.330 --> 00:54:41.360
at the very end here.

00:54:41.360 --> 00:54:45.950
So method one on Chinchilla
is to basically take

00:54:45.950 --> 00:54:48.030
the minimum over curves.

00:54:48.030 --> 00:54:49.530
And so what does that mean?

00:54:49.530 --> 00:54:52.730
Well, you basically overlay
all of the different training

00:54:52.730 --> 00:54:53.880
curves that you have.

00:54:53.880 --> 00:54:58.890
So you can see here on the
x-axis is different FLOPs.

00:54:58.890 --> 00:55:02.430
On the y-axis is
the training loss.

00:55:02.430 --> 00:55:05.610
And I have models trained
at many different sizes.

00:55:05.610 --> 00:55:07.460
And of course,
each of these sizes

00:55:07.460 --> 00:55:10.200
are going to be trained to--
with different amount of tokens.

00:55:10.200 --> 00:55:13.070
And so they're going to
reach a different total

00:55:13.070 --> 00:55:15.564
FLOP as I go through training.

00:55:15.564 --> 00:55:17.630
Now, what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:55:17.630 --> 00:55:21.650
to look at the lower envelope,
the set of points or checkpoints

00:55:21.650 --> 00:55:25.500
that proved to be optimal
under any compute budget.

00:55:25.500 --> 00:55:28.290
And I can take these models
and I can look at, OK,

00:55:28.290 --> 00:55:31.590
what were the actual parameter
sizes of these models?

00:55:31.590 --> 00:55:34.920
And you can see that the total
compute on the x-axis here

00:55:34.920 --> 00:55:37.890
and the number of parameters as
well as the corresponding tokens

00:55:37.890 --> 00:55:41.250
all forms a relatively
nice scaling law.

00:55:41.250 --> 00:55:44.430
And so this is kind of the
minimum envelope method.

00:55:44.430 --> 00:55:48.480
It's basically saying, I expect
the minimum training loss,

00:55:48.480 --> 00:55:51.650
where I optimize over all
the model sizes to actually

00:55:51.650 --> 00:55:53.270
be optimum in FLOPs.

00:55:53.270 --> 00:55:57.230
And sort of to call back to
some earlier papers, right?

00:55:57.230 --> 00:56:02.360
If you look back at the earlier
Kaplan paper and other scaling

00:56:02.360 --> 00:56:04.950
laws, you see exactly
this already being done.

00:56:04.950 --> 00:56:07.700
You see different
models being trained

00:56:07.700 --> 00:56:10.980
with different parameters
and different compute scales.

00:56:10.980 --> 00:56:13.508
And we're taking the
minimum across these.

00:56:13.508 --> 00:56:16.050
And we've already seen that the
minimum forms of scaling law.

00:56:16.050 --> 00:56:17.960
So this is building
on this observation,

00:56:17.960 --> 00:56:20.300
that the minimum across
many different training

00:56:20.300 --> 00:56:27.020
curves across compute
should form a power law.

00:56:27.020 --> 00:56:30.530
So under that assumption,
you can get fairly nice fits.

00:56:30.530 --> 00:56:33.980
And this gives you one estimate
that is quite consistent

00:56:33.980 --> 00:56:37.340
with others of 0.5, 0.5.

00:56:37.340 --> 00:56:39.410
Now, the other
one, this I think,

00:56:39.410 --> 00:56:41.900
if you were to pick a
single canonical way

00:56:41.900 --> 00:56:45.805
to do the Chinchilla analysis,
this would probably be the one.

00:56:45.805 --> 00:56:47.180
And in some ways,
I think this is

00:56:47.180 --> 00:56:50.180
the most conceptually
straightforward one, which

00:56:50.180 --> 00:56:52.050
is the IsoFLOP analysis.

00:56:52.050 --> 00:56:54.680
So to do the IsoFLOP
analysis, what you do

00:56:54.680 --> 00:56:56.580
is you pick a bunch
of compute scales.

00:56:56.580 --> 00:56:59.430
So each of these colors is a
different amount of compute.

00:56:59.430 --> 00:57:03.060
And what I'm going to do is for
each of these compute scales,

00:57:03.060 --> 00:57:06.260
I can essentially have models
with smaller parameters trained

00:57:06.260 --> 00:57:08.780
with more data or more
parameters trained

00:57:08.780 --> 00:57:09.710
with less data, right?

00:57:09.710 --> 00:57:12.560
So I'm going to sweep
over my model sizes

00:57:12.560 --> 00:57:13.940
for each of these FLOPs.

00:57:13.940 --> 00:57:17.640
And then I can look at the
minimum of each of these curves.

00:57:17.640 --> 00:57:19.920
I can either pick the
minimum point explicitly,

00:57:19.920 --> 00:57:22.670
non-parametrically, or
I could fit quadratics

00:57:22.670 --> 00:57:26.330
onto each of these and get the
minimum point of the quadratic.

00:57:26.330 --> 00:57:29.070
But in either case, the
argument is fairly simple.

00:57:29.070 --> 00:57:32.360
The argument is it should be the
case that this minimum itself

00:57:32.360 --> 00:57:34.080
follows a predictable
scaling law,

00:57:34.080 --> 00:57:39.180
and thus I can extract from it
the optimum parameters per FLOP.

00:57:39.180 --> 00:57:41.630
So that's the minimum
points across all of these.

00:57:41.630 --> 00:57:44.360
And I can also extract
the optimal number

00:57:44.360 --> 00:57:45.690
of tokens per FLOP.

00:57:45.690 --> 00:57:48.800
I can read that out by
dividing my FLOPs budget

00:57:48.800 --> 00:57:50.490
by the number of parameters.

00:57:50.490 --> 00:57:51.987
So I can get those
simultaneously.

00:57:51.987 --> 00:57:53.570
And you can see that,
once again, this

00:57:53.570 --> 00:57:58.170
gives very clean results that
are consistent with method one.

00:57:58.170 --> 00:57:59.760
So we can compare
that with before.

00:57:59.760 --> 00:58:03.140
This says for the eventual
Chinchilla model budget,

00:58:03.140 --> 00:58:05.600
you want 63 billion parameters.

00:58:05.600 --> 00:58:08.050
This one says 67
billion parameters.

00:58:08.050 --> 00:58:09.050
The two are quite close.

00:58:12.410 --> 00:58:19.070
OK, the last one, honestly,
is just a little bit messier.

00:58:19.070 --> 00:58:22.490
And this goes back to
that Rosenfeld paper.

00:58:22.490 --> 00:58:25.666
If you have a functional
form like this one,

00:58:25.666 --> 00:58:29.760
like this from Rosenfeld, a
very natural instinct is to say,

00:58:29.760 --> 00:58:33.175
I'm just going to train a bunch
of models varying both n and m,

00:58:33.175 --> 00:58:34.800
and I'm just going
to do curve fitting.

00:58:34.800 --> 00:58:36.770
I'm going fit this
curve onto whatever

00:58:36.770 --> 00:58:38.760
I get-- the thing I
get out of my model.

00:58:38.760 --> 00:58:42.530
So I'm going to train a bunch
of models and fit that 3D shape.

00:58:42.530 --> 00:58:45.650
And we know from Rosenfeld,
it's reasonable to some extent

00:58:45.650 --> 00:58:46.590
to fit these right.

00:58:46.590 --> 00:58:49.190
So you've got all these
dots, which are the models.

00:58:49.190 --> 00:58:53.360
I fitted a curve that's-- this
heat map color that you see

00:58:53.360 --> 00:58:54.650
on the left.

00:58:54.650 --> 00:58:58.490
And then you can back out what
the implied IsoFLOP should look

00:58:58.490 --> 00:59:00.860
like from these dashed lines.

00:59:00.860 --> 00:59:02.840
But if you look at
this, hopefully you

00:59:02.840 --> 00:59:06.380
see that the scaling law
fits and the curve fits here

00:59:06.380 --> 00:59:12.200
are just not quite as good as
the fits in the other plots.

00:59:12.200 --> 00:59:14.700
And if you look at
the coefficients,

00:59:14.700 --> 00:59:18.920
the Chinchilla method three just
gives way different estimates

00:59:18.920 --> 00:59:21.470
in terms of the model
size and total token count

00:59:21.470 --> 00:59:22.530
than the others.

00:59:22.530 --> 00:59:26.260
And actually, this was a
mystery to me for a long time.

00:59:26.260 --> 00:59:28.010
I think some of my
students were like, why

00:59:28.010 --> 00:59:29.220
is method three so different?

00:59:29.220 --> 00:59:30.950
And I said, I
don't maybe scaling

00:59:30.950 --> 00:59:33.290
laws are just sometimes noisy.

00:59:33.290 --> 00:59:34.980
I don't know how many
of you know this,

00:59:34.980 --> 00:59:36.950
but this is a really
fun trivia fact,

00:59:36.950 --> 00:59:40.970
or not trivia fact, fun
piece of trivia, let's say.

00:59:40.970 --> 00:59:44.487
So last year, some
folks at Epoch AI--

00:59:44.487 --> 00:59:46.070
I don't know what
motivated them to do

00:59:46.070 --> 00:59:48.950
this-- were curious
enough about this result

00:59:48.950 --> 00:59:52.550
that they went and tried
to replicate method three.

00:59:52.550 --> 00:59:56.480
And it was very
difficult to replicate it

00:59:56.480 --> 00:59:58.970
because you don't have
the original data for all

00:59:58.970 --> 01:00:00.090
of these training runs.

01:00:00.090 --> 01:00:02.390
So they actually
went to the extreme

01:00:02.390 --> 01:00:05.330
of actually looking
at the plots and using

01:00:05.330 --> 01:00:08.930
a forensic tool to extract
the values of the points

01:00:08.930 --> 01:00:09.990
from the plots.

01:00:09.990 --> 01:00:11.810
And based on that,
they could actually

01:00:11.810 --> 01:00:14.690
replicate the original result.

01:00:14.690 --> 01:00:16.190
And kind of the
funny thing is, they

01:00:16.190 --> 01:00:19.800
showed that actually the curve
fitting was the bad part.

01:00:19.800 --> 01:00:21.618
Their data in their
approach was good,

01:00:21.618 --> 01:00:23.160
but actually when
they fit the curve,

01:00:23.160 --> 01:00:24.930
they didn't necessarily
do it right.

01:00:24.930 --> 01:00:28.260
And so the original
fit had residuals.

01:00:28.260 --> 01:00:29.760
If you're familiar
with regression,

01:00:29.760 --> 01:00:31.552
your residuals should
be zero mean-centered

01:00:31.552 --> 01:00:34.610
because otherwise, you should
be offsetting your predictions

01:00:34.610 --> 01:00:36.200
to make it zero-centered.

01:00:36.200 --> 01:00:39.048
Their residuals are non-zero,
and then they fit it better.

01:00:39.048 --> 01:00:40.590
And then when they
did fit it better,

01:00:40.590 --> 01:00:45.890
well, actually their optimal
estimate almost exactly matched

01:00:45.890 --> 01:00:47.340
methods one and two.

01:00:47.340 --> 01:00:49.550
And so this is one of
those funny cases where

01:00:49.550 --> 01:00:54.000
actually the original authors
had both the idea and the data.

01:00:54.000 --> 01:00:56.722
But because of a minor
issue in curve fitting,

01:00:56.722 --> 01:00:59.180
they had kind of had it wrong,
and the replication actually

01:00:59.180 --> 01:01:01.350
makes it more
correct than before.

01:01:01.350 --> 01:01:04.303
Usually, replication disprove
things, but in this case,

01:01:04.303 --> 01:01:05.720
actually the
replication just show

01:01:05.720 --> 01:01:08.640
that the original result was
correct all along, which is,

01:01:08.640 --> 01:01:11.930
I think, a pretty cool result.

01:01:11.930 --> 01:01:15.020
OK, so the final
thing I want to talk

01:01:15.020 --> 01:01:17.960
about with this set
of Chinchilla results

01:01:17.960 --> 01:01:21.720
is we're talking about
training optimal scaling.

01:01:21.720 --> 01:01:25.590
So you have a fixed FLOPs budget
and want the best possible model

01:01:25.590 --> 01:01:26.620
possible.

01:01:26.620 --> 01:01:29.970
But really, I think that the
story has really shifted.

01:01:29.970 --> 01:01:33.570
When Chinchilla was written and
the Kaplan paper was written,

01:01:33.570 --> 01:01:36.072
LLMs were not really
a product yet.

01:01:36.072 --> 01:01:37.530
And so really, the
name of the game

01:01:37.530 --> 01:01:40.360
was everyone wanted the
most biggest, flashiest,

01:01:40.360 --> 01:01:42.455
most intelligent
model, but they didn't

01:01:42.455 --> 01:01:44.580
care about the inference
cost of actually deploying

01:01:44.580 --> 01:01:46.110
these systems.

01:01:46.110 --> 01:01:49.260
But nowadays, what
we really care about

01:01:49.260 --> 01:01:52.630
is inference costs because these
systems are actually products.

01:01:52.630 --> 01:01:53.820
They generate revenue.

01:01:53.820 --> 01:01:56.280
You have a cost associated
with the revenue.

01:01:56.280 --> 01:01:58.560
And so we've seen over
time that actually

01:01:58.560 --> 01:02:01.480
the tokens per parameter
has steadily grown right.

01:02:01.480 --> 01:02:04.740
GPT3 was 2 tokens per parameter.

01:02:04.740 --> 01:02:07.300
Chinchilla moved up to
20 tokens per parameter.

01:02:07.300 --> 01:02:09.330
And for a bit
people, played around

01:02:09.330 --> 01:02:11.650
with 20 tokens per
parameter stuff.

01:02:11.650 --> 01:02:15.210
But then very quickly,
people realized actually

01:02:15.210 --> 01:02:18.480
what we care about is really
good intelligence, that really

01:02:18.480 --> 01:02:19.780
small parameter sizes.

01:02:19.780 --> 01:02:21.160
And so people have
really started

01:02:21.160 --> 01:02:23.690
to scale up the number of
tokens per parameter very,

01:02:23.690 --> 01:02:24.790
very rapidly.

01:02:24.790 --> 01:02:27.910
And I think I saw yesterday
that, for example, the most

01:02:27.910 --> 01:02:31.400
recent Quam models were
trained on 30 trillion tokens.

01:02:31.400 --> 01:02:35.320
People are really pushing
the limits on the tokens

01:02:35.320 --> 01:02:38.410
to parameter ratio, because
really would much rather pay

01:02:38.410 --> 01:02:41.170
the up front cost than to
pay the ongoing operating

01:02:41.170 --> 01:02:48.610
cost of running inference on
a really big, expensive model.

01:02:48.610 --> 01:02:50.620
Cool.

01:02:50.620 --> 01:02:54.700
Last thing that is kind
of a fun side thing

01:02:54.700 --> 01:02:58.030
that I want to end
with is to say,

01:02:58.030 --> 01:03:01.000
these results are pretty
robust and easy to replicate.

01:03:01.000 --> 01:03:03.260
A few years back,
one of my students,

01:03:03.260 --> 01:03:07.270
Ishan, was really interested in
really pushing diffusion models

01:03:07.270 --> 01:03:08.150
for text forward.

01:03:08.150 --> 01:03:10.400
And so one of the things
that we had to do was to say,

01:03:10.400 --> 01:03:11.990
this is a whole new model.

01:03:11.990 --> 01:03:14.510
We don't know what the optimal
token to parameter ratio is.

01:03:14.510 --> 01:03:17.120
We don't know if this
thing even reliably scales.

01:03:17.120 --> 01:03:19.830
It's a totally different
kind of generative model.

01:03:19.830 --> 01:03:20.980
What do we do?

01:03:20.980 --> 01:03:25.170
Well, turns out if you just
fit the same kind of playbook

01:03:25.170 --> 01:03:28.020
of saying, oh, we're going
to do IsoFLOP analysis

01:03:28.020 --> 01:03:30.240
for autoregressive models,
we get almost exactly

01:03:30.240 --> 01:03:32.470
the Chinchilla thing
without too much effort.

01:03:32.470 --> 01:03:35.370
You do the same kind of analysis
on diffusion models, wow,

01:03:35.370 --> 01:03:37.353
we see very similar
kinds of curves,

01:03:37.353 --> 01:03:39.270
even though it's a pretty
different generative

01:03:39.270 --> 01:03:40.300
model entirely.

01:03:40.300 --> 01:03:42.970
And then if you plot the
minimum across of these,

01:03:42.970 --> 01:03:44.940
well, you see very
predictable scaling

01:03:44.940 --> 01:03:47.410
for both separated
by a constant offset.

01:03:47.410 --> 01:03:51.150
I don't bring this up to say,
because I want to particularly

01:03:51.150 --> 01:03:55.500
push to you diffusion models,
but just as a really random case

01:03:55.500 --> 01:03:57.972
study or example to
say, these scaling laws

01:03:57.972 --> 01:04:00.180
don't necessarily need to
be these very cherry-picked

01:04:00.180 --> 01:04:01.030
examples.

01:04:01.030 --> 01:04:03.300
They seem to happen
pretty naturally

01:04:03.300 --> 01:04:06.730
as you're working on new models
or working on new environments.

01:04:10.620 --> 01:04:17.760
OK, this is to put
together this last part.

01:04:17.760 --> 01:04:21.330
Log linearity is not just
about one dimensional things

01:04:21.330 --> 01:04:22.510
where we think about data.

01:04:22.510 --> 01:04:24.130
They extend to model parameters.

01:04:24.130 --> 01:04:25.720
They extend to total compute.

01:04:25.720 --> 01:04:28.710
And so that lets us make
all sorts of hyperparameter

01:04:28.710 --> 01:04:29.860
and other decisions.

01:04:29.860 --> 01:04:31.620
That's this first part.

01:04:31.620 --> 01:04:34.690
And they're also letting us make
really smart resource trade.

01:04:34.690 --> 01:04:37.530
They let us make trade offs
between big models versus more

01:04:37.530 --> 01:04:38.820
data.

01:04:38.820 --> 01:04:41.770
And we saw that in this
Chinchilla analysis.

01:04:41.770 --> 01:04:45.930
And it's kind of remarkable how
cleanly things like the IsoFLOP

01:04:45.930 --> 01:04:49.890
analysis turn out.

01:04:49.890 --> 01:04:53.670
All right, that's all I got
for basic data or basic scaling

01:04:53.670 --> 01:04:54.450
laws.

01:04:54.450 --> 01:04:58.780
We did a recap of Kaplan as
well as Chinchilla today.

01:04:58.780 --> 01:05:00.660
And hopefully now,
you're on board

01:05:00.660 --> 01:05:03.450
with this idea of data
scaling, model scaling,

01:05:03.450 --> 01:05:05.580
and using scaling
laws to optimize

01:05:05.580 --> 01:05:07.770
all the aspects of your
model without actually

01:05:07.770 --> 01:05:10.590
going all the way to the
large scale training runs.

01:05:10.590 --> 01:05:13.340
Thanks, and I'll see
you all Thursday.
